WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:02.510 line:84%
Welcome to the first
Academy Dialogues.

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:05.570 line:84%
My name is Shawn Finnie from
the Member Relations Department.

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:08.360 line:84%
Academy Dialogues is a new
series of online conversations

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.770 line:90%
hosted by the Academy.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:11.780 line:10%
It begins today with the
Power of Narrative --

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:14.810 line:10%
a conversation between Academy
Governor Whoopi Goldberg

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.120 line:10%
and civil rights
lawyer Bryan Stevenson,

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.970 line:10%
founder and executive
director of the Equal Justice

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:20.870 line:10%
Initiative.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:22.930 line:10%
They'll be discussing
Hollywood's historic place

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.663 line:10%
as the purveyor of ethnic
and racial stereotypes,

00:00:25.663 --> 00:00:27.080 line:10%
and how the power
of narrative can

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.411 line:10%
become the heart of recovery
by addressing racial, ethnic,

00:00:30.411 --> 00:00:32.119 line:10%
and gender inequities
through the stories

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:34.100 line:10%
we choose to tell now.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.170 line:10%
Our first guest needs
little introduction.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.860 line:10%
Recognized around the world,
actor, performer, activist,

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.500 line:10%
and co-host of ABC
talk show, The View,

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.200 line:10%
where she guides conversation
across American politics,

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.680 line:10%
race relations, popular
culture, and everyday life--

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.620 line:10%
Oscar, Emmy, Grammy,
and Tony Award

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:56.012 line:10%
winner, and Academy
Governor, Whoopi Goldberg.

00:00:56.012 --> 00:00:57.000 line:10%
Hello.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.070 line:10%
Our next guest is
Bryan Stevenson.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.680 line:10%
Bryan is a graduate of
both Harvard Law School

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.500 line:10%
and Harvard's John F.
Kennedy School of Government.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.530 line:10%
In 1989, he founded the
Equal Justice Initiative

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.900 line:10%
and remains its
executive director.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.330 line:10%
EJI is based in
Alabama and focuses

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.750 line:10%
on social justice, human rights,
and criminal justice reform

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.190 line:10%
in the United States.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:20.910 line:10%
He has argued five cases at
the Supreme Court and won four.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:24.060 line:10%
His book, Just Mercy: A Story
of Justice and Redemption

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.710 line:10%
is the basis for the
2019 film, Just Mercy,

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:31.590 line:10%
that profiles wrongfully
accused death row victim, Walter

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:32.820 line:10%
McMillan.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.880 line:10%
Please welcome Bryan Stevenson.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.210 line:90%
It's great to be with you.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:39.590 line:90%
Actually, you want to jump in?

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.800 line:84%
You want to just
jump right into it?

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:43.520 line:90%
Let's do it.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.990 line:84%
Well, in listening to all the
things that you've been doing,

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.840 line:84%
I want to know
just from you, how

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:55.550 line:84%
do you think we got here
at this particular moment?

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.230 line:84%
It's a really
important question,

00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:59.480 line:84%
because I don't think
we've done a very good job

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.460 line:84%
in this country of
educating people

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.250 line:90%
about the history of America.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:09.470 line:84%
And we are a country
that has been

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:13.160 line:84%
corrupted by a history
of racial injustice.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.920 line:84%
I believe we're not
really free, because we

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.570 line:84%
haven't talked about
the things that

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.550 line:90%
have created this environment.

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:21.890 line:84%
We have a history
of racial inequality

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:25.880 line:84%
that is so toxic
that you can still

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.150 line:90%
feel it and see it in the air.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.630 line:84%
There's a smog in
the atmosphere.

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.670 line:10%
And so I believe
that we really have

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.560 line:10%
to educate ourselves
about the fact

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.270 line:10%
that we're a
post-genocide society.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.400 line:84%
I think what happened
to Native people

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.340 line:84%
when Europeans came to this
continent was a genocide.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.100 line:84%
We killed millions
of Indigenous people

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.750 line:84%
through famine and
war and disease,

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.090 line:84%
but we never acknowledge
that violence.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.580 line:84%
We kept their words-- half the
states in America are Native

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:50.420 line:90%
words--

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:53.438 line:84%
but we made those people
leave and we took their land.

00:02:53.438 --> 00:02:54.980 line:84%
We created a
Constitution that talked

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.540 line:84%
about equality and
justice for all,

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.150 line:90%
but excluded these populations.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.010 line:84%
And that narrative
of racial difference

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.920 line:84%
that we created to
justify that violence

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.282 line:84%
is what then gave rise to 2
and 1/2 centuries of slavery.

00:03:08.282 --> 00:03:09.740 line:84%
And the thing that
I've been trying

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:11.780 line:84%
to emphasize with people
is that I don't think

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:13.940 line:84%
the true evil of
American slavery

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:16.320 line:84%
was involuntary servitude
and forced labor.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.620 line:84%
I think the real
evil of slavery was

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:21.308 line:84%
the narrative we created
that Black people aren't

00:03:21.308 --> 00:03:23.600 line:84%
as good as white people, that
Black people aren't fully

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.330 line:84%
human, that Black people
are less deserving, less

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:28.160 line:90%
evolved, less human.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.980 line:84%
And that's a terribly
destructive narrative

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.510 line:84%
to create for a
whole race of people.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.410 line:84%
And we fight the Civil
War, and the 13th Amendment

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:38.630 line:84%
is passed to end
bondage, but it doesn't

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:42.650 line:84%
address that fundamental
narrative, that ideology

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:43.880 line:90%
of white supremacy.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:48.150 line:84%
Because of that, I don't
think slavery ends in 1865.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:49.970 line:90%
I think it evolves.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:54.320 line:84%
And then we see a century
of lynching and terrorism

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.380 line:84%
and segregation and racism
and racial hierarchy.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:01.790 line:84%
And we've never really focused
on overcoming that narrative,

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:04.160 line:84%
confronting this ideology
of white supremacy,

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.430 line:84%
dealing with the
damage created by 400

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:09.980 line:90%
years of racist thinking.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.430 line:84%
And because we
haven't done that,

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.570 line:84%
we now live in a moment, where
Black and brown people are

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:16.490 line:90%
presumed dangerous or guilty.

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:20.103 line:84%
You can be a talented
Oscar-winning actress or actor,

00:04:20.103 --> 00:04:22.520 line:84%
but if you're Black, you will
go places where you're going

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.892 line:84%
to have to navigate presumptions
of dangerousness and guilt.

00:04:25.892 --> 00:04:28.350 line:84%
And that's why so many people
are out on the streets today,

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:30.255 line:84%
because when you have
to live like that,

00:04:30.255 --> 00:04:31.880 line:84%
no matter how much
you've accomplished,

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.260 line:84%
no matter what you've done,
how much study you've done --

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.590 line:84%
when you still have to
go places and be presumed

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:40.700 line:84%
dangerous and guilty because of
your color, it gets exhausting.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.410 line:90%
You get tired.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:45.620 line:84%
And a lot of us are feeling
the weight of that history,

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:46.640 line:90%
that exhaustion.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.720 line:90%
And we want things to change.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.240 line:84%
And I think that's
how we got here.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.910 line:84%
We got here by being
silent about our history,

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:56.270 line:84%
about ignoring and
denying the past that

00:04:56.270 --> 00:05:00.387 line:84%
has so fundamentally undermined
and compromised our present.

00:05:00.387 --> 00:05:02.720 line:84%
And that's why I do believe
this is a moment where we're

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:04.470 line:84%
going to have to reckon
with that history,

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.590 line:84%
talk about Native genocide,
talk about slavery,

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:08.990 line:84%
talk about lynching,
talk about segregation,

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:12.350 line:84%
talk about racial inequality,
talk about police violence,

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:15.830 line:84%
talk about narrative failures
in Hollywood and entertainment.

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:19.010 line:84%
Talk about all of these things
to create a new understanding

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:21.740 line:84%
and a new commitment
to what it means

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:23.600 line:90%
to live in a just society.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.640 line:84%
Well, one of the
interesting things for me

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:30.470 line:84%
is whenever I watched
television as a kid,

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:32.780 line:84%
there were narratives
that were there

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:36.950 line:84%
that are in me, because
that's what you saw.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:39.410 line:84%
So you just assumed,
why would they lie?

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:43.230 line:90%
Must've been like this.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:43.980 line:90%
What wasn't?

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:48.530 line:84%
And then suddenly, you start
to learn that secondary history

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:50.690 line:90%
that people don't talk about.

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:52.520 line:84%
A lot of my friends
who are Italian

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:56.420 line:84%
have no idea that one of the
largest lynchings in the United

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:58.505 line:84%
States of America
was of Italians.

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:05.630 line:84%
My Irish friends, they don't
have a real full understanding

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:08.840 line:84%
that they too were
looked down upon.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:13.340 line:84%
And if we start talking about
the indigenous people here,

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:17.840 line:84%
these are all narratives that
have been shifted and changed

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.220 line:90%
and moved around.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:23.330 line:84%
And it's why adults,
people my age,

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:27.260 line:84%
kind of look at things and
kind of go, well, where am I?

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:28.550 line:90%
Where am I in this?

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:31.850 line:84%
I think people older
than me probably

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:34.910 line:84%
thought about it, but
not in the same way.

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:39.080 line:90%
I love surf movies and I never--

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:42.770 line:84%
we're not even walking
past on the beach.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:46.080 line:90%
We're not anywhere near.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.140 line:90%
Or Where the Boys Are.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.940 line:84%
Well, where were the boys
that I was looking for?

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.050 line:90%
That's right.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:54.500 line:84%
Because we're all going
through the same thing,

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:56.990 line:84%
but it looks like
we're not present.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:57.590 line:90%
That's right.

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:00.530 line:84%
And those narrative
choices for me

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:03.530 line:84%
are really important,
because when

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:06.830 line:84%
you tell a story like that,
when you make a film like that,

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:08.410 line:90%
you're making choices.

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:10.400 line:84%
And when you're excluding
whole populations,

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.160 line:84%
you're saying something
about the worth and value

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:16.430 line:90%
of those populations.

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:18.380 line:84%
Throughout the first
half of the 20th century,

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:20.755 line:84%
thousands of Black people were
pulled out of their homes.

00:07:20.755 --> 00:07:23.005 line:84%
They were beaten, they were
drowned, they were hanged,

00:07:23.005 --> 00:07:23.810 line:90%
they were tortured.

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:25.670 line:84%
And these lynchings
that would sometimes

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:28.880 line:84%
attract 1,000 or
5,000 white people,

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:31.400 line:84%
and they would be happening
on the courthouse lawn.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:34.250 line:84%
And our law enforcement agencies
and the federal government

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:35.940 line:90%
said nothing and did nothing.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:38.780 line:84%
So when you tell
stories about that era,

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.630 line:84%
and you don't talk
about that phenomenon,

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:43.610 line:84%
it's almost as if the
lives of Black people

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:47.002 line:84%
are irrelevant to the first
half of the 20th century.

00:07:47.002 --> 00:07:48.710 line:84%
We made all of these
stories and films --

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:51.920 line:84%
I grew up watching
these cowboy westerns

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:55.150 line:84%
and Native people were presented
as villains, as ruthless.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:56.060 line:90%
Always.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:58.970 line:84%
They never had full
identities and characters.

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:02.180 line:84%
And we were reinforcing this
narrative of racial difference.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:05.600 line:84%
This idea that Native people
and Indigenous people,

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:06.433 line:90%
they're not like us.

00:08:06.433 --> 00:08:07.433 line:90%
They're not fully human.

00:08:07.433 --> 00:08:08.540 line:90%
They're not to be valued.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:13.520 line:84%
And that choice gives
support to this history.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:15.080 line:84%
And so now we're
having to reckon

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:19.700 line:90%
with all of that miseducation.

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:22.640 line:84%
There are two statutes,
one was in Central Park,

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:25.390 line:84%
one is outside the
capitol in Alabama,

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:27.380 line:90%
of this man named Marion Sims.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:28.430 line:90%
He was a doctor.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:30.620 line:84%
In medical schools, they
frequently refer to him

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:33.049 line:90%
as the father of gynecology.

00:08:33.049 --> 00:08:36.320 line:84%
And what he did in central
Alabama, in the 1850s,

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.600 line:90%
was experiment on Black women.

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:39.260 line:90%
Yeah.

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:41.299 line:84%
He didn't believe that
Black people felt pain.

00:08:41.299 --> 00:08:43.091 line:84%
So he would actually
pull these Black women

00:08:43.091 --> 00:08:44.660 line:84%
and start cutting
on their bodies,

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:48.040 line:84%
and they would be writhing
and screaming in agony.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:49.700 line:84%
And he thought they
were pretending.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:54.710 line:84%
And he made these discoveries
about the birth process

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:57.740 line:84%
that he could then use
and provide some better

00:08:57.740 --> 00:08:58.790 line:90%
outcomes for white women.

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:00.165 line:84%
Because of those
better outcomes,

00:09:00.165 --> 00:09:02.850 line:84%
he was hailed as
this medical hero.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:05.100 line:84%
And there's a statue
of him in Central Park.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:07.870 line:84%
There's a statue of him in
front of the Alabama capitol.

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:10.880 line:84%
Well, the truth is
that he was horrific.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:12.320 line:90%
He tortured Black women.

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:15.440 line:90%
He experimented on Black bodies.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:18.560 line:84%
And it was completely
dishonorable what he did.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:21.230 line:84%
You cannot honor someone who
did something dishonorable,

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:23.810 line:84%
so the statue has come
down in Central Park.

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:26.070 line:84%
But just a few years
ago, that statue

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:27.590 line:84%
was still outside
of our capitol.

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:29.660 line:84%
When you begin to
understand these histories,

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:32.150 line:84%
you think differently
about what's honorable.

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:34.510 line:84%
And we've honored things in
our films and our stories

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:37.010 line:84%
and in our TV programming that
we should have never honored.

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:39.230 line:84%
We should have been
more honest about what

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:40.910 line:90%
those people represented.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:44.810 line:84%
And also, it doesn't
help when you have people

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:47.800 line:90%
in makeup playing those folks.

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:56.010 line:84%
And you think to yourself, well,
were there no Native Americans?

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:59.080 line:90%
You couldn't find anybody?

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:02.860 line:84%
Jay Silverheels
didn't -- you know?

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:07.276 line:84%
Because when you start to
tell the narrative as it,

00:10:07.276 --> 00:10:10.190 line:90%
it becomes an imbalance.

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:10.690 line:90%
Yes.

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:14.350 line:90%
Because it started out that way.

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:16.590 line:84%
It's never been a
balanced playing field.

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:17.260 line:90%
Not ever.

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:20.627 line:84%
Not for Native Americans,
not for all the Asian folks

00:10:20.627 --> 00:10:22.210 line:84%
who came over to
work in this country.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:25.130 line:84%
It's never been
equal or balanced.

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:28.780 line:84%
And when I look at
what's happening now,

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:33.310 line:84%
people are saying,
listen, you can tell me

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:37.030 line:84%
who you think your
heroes are, but I'm

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:40.270 line:84%
here and been here
long enough to tell you

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:42.520 line:84%
your heroes may
not be my heroes.

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:45.860 line:84%
And so I want a part
of the conversation.

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:47.590 line:84%
And I think that's
what people are doing

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:53.900 line:84%
and have been doing over the
last 10, 15 years with cinema.

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:56.440 line:84%
People want to be
present, they want

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:59.950 line:84%
to be part of this
cinema that's happening

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:06.160 line:84%
without the sort of ideas
that people have thrown out

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:06.970 line:90%
from time to time.

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:08.810 line:84%
Like, well, if you
have a whole bunch

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:11.630 line:84%
of folks of color in a movie,
it's not gonna sell overseas.

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:15.020 line:90%
Actually, they do.

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:17.885 line:90%
I'm a perfect example.

00:11:17.885 --> 00:11:21.450 line:84%
I'm hotter there
than here, you know?

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:21.950 line:90%
Yeah.

00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:25.070 line:84%
And what people don't
appreciate is -- it's funny,

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:27.950 line:84%
because it's such
a misguided notion.

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.680 line:84%
In World War I,
when Black soldiers

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:34.335 line:84%
went to France and Germany,
they were embraced,

00:11:34.335 --> 00:11:35.210 line:90%
they were celebrated.

00:11:35.210 --> 00:11:39.710 line:84%
Black veterans got more
recognition, more applause,

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:41.330 line:84%
more acknowledgment
for what they

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:43.490 line:84%
did during World War I
in Europe than they ever

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:45.050 line:90%
did in this country.

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:48.060 line:84%
When they came back from World
War I wearing their uniforms,

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:50.335 line:84%
they were actually
targeted for violence.

00:11:50.335 --> 00:11:52.460 line:10%
And we have a report that
talks about the high rate

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:55.088 line:10%
of lynchings of Black
veterans after World War I,

00:11:55.088 --> 00:11:56.630 line:10%
because white
Southerners didn't want

00:11:56.630 --> 00:11:58.480 line:10%
them to believe that
they were now somebody

00:11:58.480 --> 00:11:59.480 line:10%
because they had fought.

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:01.970 line:84%
But their European
experience actually

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:03.920 line:84%
challenged the idea
that they were less

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:07.190 line:90%
than their white compatriots.

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:09.440 line:84%
Same thing happens
in World War II.

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:11.690 line:84%
And throughout the
1950s and '60s,

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:13.730 line:84%
actually, Black artists
would go to Europe,

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:16.700 line:84%
because they felt like
that was a space where they

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:20.218 line:84%
could avoid or at least
minimize some of the burdens

00:12:20.218 --> 00:12:20.760 line:90%
and barriers.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:22.700 line:84%
So this idea that you
can't tell stories

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:24.890 line:84%
about people of
color that are going

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:27.860 line:84%
to be embraced or understood
in the international markets

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:29.750 line:84%
is a very confusing
one when you actually

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:32.930 line:84%
look at the history of
international and European

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:35.570 line:84%
responses to the
Black experience.

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:37.580 line:84%
And I do think it's so
important what you said

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:39.620 line:90%
about the absence of diversity.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:43.040 line:84%
I mean, you couldn't have
made those films about cowboys

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:47.600 line:84%
and Indians if you had
real Native people on set,

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:50.510 line:84%
because they would be
challenging these portrayals.

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:52.872 line:84%
They would complicate
the ease with which

00:12:52.872 --> 00:12:54.080 line:90%
these stories are being told.

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:57.460 line:84%
And I think so much of that
would be true for Black people,

00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:57.960 line:90%
as well.

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.553 line:84%
And this is funny, because
I talk to my friends,

00:13:00.553 --> 00:13:02.720 line:84%
and they'll say whenever
they see these stories that

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:06.340 line:84%
are supposed to be about
the experience of women,

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:09.660 line:84%
I can tell there are no women
involved in that storymaking.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.630 line:84%
No women were involved
in the creative process,

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:15.390 line:84%
because the story doesn't ring
true to the lives of women.

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:17.990 line:84%
And that's why
diversity and inclusion

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:21.200 line:84%
is such a critical
component of how we begin

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:23.570 line:90%
changing these narratives.

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:25.710 line:84%
People talk about diversity,
but a lot of times,

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:28.610 line:84%
they just want women and
people of color who think

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:30.843 line:84%
and act just like they
do to be in the room.

00:13:30.843 --> 00:13:32.510 line:84%
They want a different
color to the room,

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:33.830 line:84%
they want a different
gender to the room,

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:35.240 line:84%
but they don't want
different thinking.

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:35.740 line:90%
Yes.

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:37.720 line:84%
We are not going to
get to the just society

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:40.310 line:84%
unless we open our minds
and our hearts to the idea

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:42.673 line:84%
that as things
diversify, things change.

00:13:42.673 --> 00:13:44.840 line:84%
The thinking is going to
change, the conversation is

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:46.850 line:84%
going to change,
the storytelling is

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:47.580 line:90%
going to change.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:50.420 line:84%
It's going to be more authentic,
and honest, and realistic,

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:51.680 line:90%
and informed.

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:53.330 line:84%
And that's the
power of diversity

00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:55.400 line:84%
at this moment in all
sectors, but certainly

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:57.170 line:90%
in the storytelling sector.

00:13:57.170 --> 00:13:59.720 line:84%
It's an interesting
word, diversity.

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:01.730 line:84%
Because people
throw it out, they

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:03.690 line:90%
say, yes, we want to be diverse.

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:06.350 line:84%
But I don't know that
people really understand

00:14:06.350 --> 00:14:08.480 line:90%
what that actually means.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:13.850 line:84%
Because when you say to someone,
well, if you had diversity here

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:18.260 line:84%
in your newsroom,
someone might have said,

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:22.070 line:84%
we don't need to watch
another eight minute

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:24.560 line:90%
segment of this man dying.

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:26.010 line:90%
Mm-hmm.

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:27.770 line:90%
Because he's got family.

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:31.040 line:84%
So where you have people
who are saying, "wait, wait,

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:32.940 line:84%
we know we're trying
to tell this story,

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:35.570 line:84%
but we have now shown this
over and over and over

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:36.720 line:90%
and over and over."

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:40.400 line:84%
So I need you to figure
out another way for us

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:42.770 line:90%
to continue to do it.

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:47.240 line:84%
It's little things and I think
it's little things for women

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:50.860 line:84%
that women would think of
that a guy just would not.

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:52.160 line:90%
Yeah.

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:56.090 line:84%
People of color will recognize
and see, I've seen that,

00:14:56.090 --> 00:14:59.030 line:84%
and we need to do
this differently.

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:02.720 line:84%
There are just little tweaks
that people can help make.

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:12.230 line:84%
Why do you think people
are afraid to really have

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:13.640 line:90%
this conversation?

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:16.130 line:84%
What is it that really
is scaring people?

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:19.280 line:84%
Yeah, I think we have
practiced silence

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:22.400 line:84%
for so long that
when people start

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:25.900 line:84%
saying the things that you
know haven't been said before,

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:27.470 line:90%
it unnerves people.

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:30.620 line:84%
And I do think
people are afraid.

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:35.390 line:84%
We've got politicians who have
governed through the politics

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:36.980 line:90%
of fear and anger.

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:40.435 line:84%
And we've been taught, many
of us, that if that happens,

00:15:40.435 --> 00:15:41.810 line:84%
something bad will
happen to you.

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:43.220 line:90%
If that happens, something bad--

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:46.250 line:84%
we got into mass
incarceration in the 1970s

00:15:46.250 --> 00:15:49.770 line:84%
because we had politicians that
were preaching fear and anger.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:51.327 line:84%
And they said, we
need a war on drugs

00:15:51.327 --> 00:15:53.660 line:84%
because people who are drug
addicted and drug dependent,

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:54.935 line:90%
they're dangerous.

00:15:54.935 --> 00:15:56.560 line:84%
We're going to treat
them as criminals.

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:57.950 line:84%
Now we could have said
that people dealing

00:15:57.950 --> 00:16:00.260 line:84%
with addiction and dependency
have a health problem,

00:16:00.260 --> 00:16:01.740 line:90%
we need a health care response.

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:04.880 line:84%
We don't have to be afraid--
we just have to be effective.

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:08.300 line:84%
But that politics of fear
and anger was very powerful.

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:10.910 line:84%
I think fear and anger are
the essential ingredients

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:13.070 line:90%
of injustice and inequality.

00:16:13.070 --> 00:16:15.660 line:84%
And we've done a lot of
that in this country.

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:18.345 line:84%
When you look at the history
of slavery and even the history

00:16:18.345 --> 00:16:19.720 line:84%
of what happened
to Indigenous --

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:22.250 line:84%
you have to be afraid of
Indigenous people and Native

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:24.030 line:90%
people, they'll kill you.

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:25.583 line:84%
You have to be
afraid of Black men,

00:16:25.583 --> 00:16:27.250 line:84%
they'll do all of
these terrible things.

00:16:27.250 --> 00:16:30.590 line:84%
It was this fear of full
equality that shaped that,

00:16:30.590 --> 00:16:33.050 line:84%
and that's why those lynchings
were so powerful and potent.

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:35.330 line:84%
We can't let our children
go to school with Black kids

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:38.160 line:84%
because that wouldn't
be safe and healthy.

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:41.210 line:84%
And I think that as
we begin now to tell

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:44.900 line:84%
the truth about this history,
we have to just have courage.

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:48.570 line:84%
It's interesting when you think
about the elements of therapy,

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:51.410 line:84%
whether you're dealing
with addiction, alcoholism,

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:53.977 line:84%
drug addiction, whatever -- the
first thing you have to do is

00:16:53.977 --> 00:16:56.060 line:84%
to be willing to tell the
truth about who you are.

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:58.070 line:84%
12 steps works,
only works, when you

00:16:58.070 --> 00:17:01.088 line:84%
are prepared to say, "I'm an
alcoholic, I'm an addict."

00:17:01.088 --> 00:17:03.380 line:84%
And then you begin to tell
the truth of what that means

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:04.910 line:84%
in your life, and
that truth telling

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:07.940 line:84%
is an essential
part of the process.

00:17:07.940 --> 00:17:10.670 line:84%
And what we have to have the
courage to have in this country

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:15.530 line:84%
is to admit that we are a nation
shaped by racism and bigotry.

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:17.599 line:90%
We are a post-genocide society.

00:17:17.599 --> 00:17:18.890 line:90%
We've done horrific things.

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:20.540 line:90%
We were a slave society.

00:17:20.540 --> 00:17:23.000 line:84%
We were a society that
perpetrated racial terrorism.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:27.290 line:84%
The Black people that are
in the North and the West

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:29.510 line:84%
are consequences of
this era of terror.

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:33.020 line:84%
Between 1900 and 1950,
millions of Black people

00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:34.110 line:90%
fled the American South.

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:36.020 line:84%
And we haven't
acknowledged the fact

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:38.060 line:84%
that the Black people
in Cleveland and Chicago

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:41.300 line:84%
and Detroit and Los Angeles and
Oakland and New York and Boston

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:44.240 line:84%
didn't go to those communities
as immigrants looking

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:45.720 line:90%
for new economic opportunities.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:48.230 line:84%
They went to those communities
as refugees and exiles

00:17:48.230 --> 00:17:50.148 line:84%
from terror in the
American South.

00:17:50.148 --> 00:17:51.440 line:90%
And we haven't talked about it.

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:53.210 line:84%
And so all the Black
people come up to me

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:55.082 line:84%
and they say "Mr.
Stevenson, I get angry

00:17:55.082 --> 00:17:56.540 line:10%
when I hear somebody
say that we're

00:17:56.540 --> 00:17:58.623 line:10%
dealing with domestic
terrorism for the first time

00:17:58.623 --> 00:18:00.613 line:10%
in our nation's
history after 9/11."

00:18:00.613 --> 00:18:02.030 line:84%
They say, "we grew
up with terror.

00:18:02.030 --> 00:18:03.890 line:84%
We have to worry about being
bombed and lynched and menaced

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:05.330 line:90%
every day of our life."

00:18:05.330 --> 00:18:08.300 line:84%
And because we have
this fear that if we're

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:11.870 line:84%
honest about the past, it'll
be painful and difficult,

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:13.250 line:90%
we try to avoid it.

00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:16.460 line:84%
But the reality is, is what
every therapeutic intervention

00:18:16.460 --> 00:18:18.200 line:84%
that's effective
that I know tells

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:21.920 line:84%
us is that it's only when we're
honest that we get better.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:23.930 line:84%
It's through truth-telling
that we can actually

00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:27.830 line:84%
begin to understand how to
cope with our addictions,

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:30.680 line:90%
our dependency, our abuse.

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:31.742 line:90%
I mean, an abuse --

00:18:31.742 --> 00:18:33.950 line:84%
a lot of time for the first
half of the 20th century,

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:36.260 line:84%
said people who were sex
abuse victims and child abuse

00:18:36.260 --> 00:18:39.860 line:84%
victims should never talk about
it, and that's not healthy.

00:18:39.860 --> 00:18:42.440 line:84%
So I think that's what we have
to get people to understand --

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:45.110 line:84%
is that if we have the
courage to tell the truth --

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:47.660 line:84%
I mean, I'm from a faith
tradition, in my church,

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:49.220 line:84%
you can't just
walk in and say, "I

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:50.720 line:84%
want heaven and all
that good stuff,

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:53.270 line:84%
I want all of that
redemption and salvation."

00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:55.670 line:84%
They'll tell you, well,
you first have to repent.

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:56.950 line:90%
You have to confess.

00:18:56.950 --> 00:19:00.200 line:10%
You cannot be afraid to tell
the truth of what you've done

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:01.700 line:10%
and who you are.

00:19:01.700 --> 00:19:03.290 line:84%
Once you do that,
on the other side

00:19:03.290 --> 00:19:06.190 line:84%
is redemption and
restoration and uplift

00:19:06.190 --> 00:19:07.550 line:90%
and all of these things.

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:09.555 line:84%
And part of what I'm
trying to do in our work

00:19:09.555 --> 00:19:11.180 line:84%
is to tell people,
I don't want to talk

00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:13.850 line:84%
about the Native genocide
and slavery and lynching

00:19:13.850 --> 00:19:17.065 line:84%
and segregation because
I want to punish America.

00:19:17.065 --> 00:19:18.440 line:84%
I want to talk
about these things

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:20.240 line:90%
because I want to liberate us.

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:22.800 line:84%
I want us to get
to something else.

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:26.000 line:84%
And I really believe
that there's something

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:27.240 line:90%
better waiting for us.

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:29.450 line:84%
I think there is
something that feels more

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:32.120 line:84%
like freedom, that feels more
like equality, that feels more

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:33.290 line:90%
like justice.

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:35.420 line:84%
There are better
movies to be made.

00:19:35.420 --> 00:19:37.500 line:84%
There are better
stories to be told.

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:40.640 line:84%
There's a kind of future
for this industry that

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:44.048 line:84%
energizes all people, because
it tells all of those stories.

00:19:44.048 --> 00:19:45.590 line:84%
But to get there,
we're going to have

00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:48.200 line:84%
to be honest about the
ways we have failed,

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:51.320 line:84%
the ways we have
undermined truth telling,

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:52.800 line:90%
and then give truth to that.

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:54.890 line:84%
And for me, truth and
justice is sequential.

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:56.690 line:84%
People want to skip
to the reconciliation,

00:19:56.690 --> 00:19:58.040 line:84%
they want to skip
to the reparation

00:19:58.040 --> 00:19:59.123 line:90%
without telling the truth.

00:19:59.123 --> 00:20:00.290 line:90%
It doesn't work like that.

00:20:00.290 --> 00:20:02.000 line:84%
It's only when we
tell the truth that we

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:04.970 line:84%
have a consciousness that
allows us to repair the damage,

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:09.940 line:84%
to restore confidence, to
build healthy relationships.

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:11.830 line:84%
And I'm going to
ask this question,

00:20:11.830 --> 00:20:17.410 line:84%
and I hope it's taken in
the spirit that I ask it.

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:19.060 line:84%
What do we need
to help people --

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:20.410 line:90%
white people -- understand?

00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:22.980 line:84%
Because I feel like white
people are very nervous.

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:23.480 line:90%
Yeah.

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:26.920 line:90%
They're very nervous.

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:32.120 line:84%
Because sometimes, I feel
like, you should know this.

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:34.900 line:84%
I should not be walking
you through this.

00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:38.620 line:84%
And then I realize that
unless a lot of this

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:41.680 line:84%
has touched you in
your life, you're

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:44.050 line:84%
not going to be thinking
about it the way that I am.

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:47.020 line:84%
You're not going to
notice where I am not.

00:20:47.020 --> 00:20:47.590 line:90%
Yeah.

00:20:47.590 --> 00:20:54.370 line:84%
So what can we do to help people
who are not people of color?

00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:55.870 line:90%
Who are not women?

00:20:55.870 --> 00:21:00.580 line:84%
Who are white
people or white men?

00:21:00.580 --> 00:21:04.890 line:84%
How do we help them
walk through this storm?

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:07.210 line:90%
Because it is a storm.

00:21:07.210 --> 00:21:07.710 line:90%
Yeah.

00:21:07.710 --> 00:21:11.100 line:84%
Well, this is where I think
actually Hollywood and film

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:13.620 line:84%
and media have a
critical role to play,

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:16.140 line:84%
because what this
industry can do,

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:19.980 line:84%
which very few industries
can do, is to invite people

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:22.800 line:84%
into a space, and in
a couple of hours,

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:25.947 line:84%
tell a story that radically
shifts their thinking.

00:21:25.947 --> 00:21:28.530 line:84%
And I've seen it, just because
my book was turned into a movie

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:29.490 line:90%
--

00:21:29.490 --> 00:21:30.930 line:90%
movie came out in January --

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:34.620 line:84%
I've gotten thousands and
thousands of emails and letters

00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:38.250 line:84%
from people who were saying,
"I'm a 57-year-old white man,

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:39.210 line:90%
I'm a 70 --

00:21:39.210 --> 00:21:41.698 line:84%
I did not understand
the problems

00:21:41.698 --> 00:21:43.740 line:84%
that were being created
by our criminal justice."

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:45.900 line:84%
And they desperately now
want to do something.

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:48.540 line:84%
And they just saw a movie, and
then they want to read a book,

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:50.450 line:84%
and now they want
to see other things.

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:53.340 line:84%
Storytelling is a powerful
way to invite people

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:56.587 line:84%
into a conversation that
wouldn't ordinarily get there.

00:21:56.587 --> 00:21:58.170 line:84%
And we've done it
over and over again.

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.210 line:84%
I think about domestic
violence for example.

00:22:00.210 --> 00:22:02.360 line:84%
And your question,
Whoopi, about white men,

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:04.660 line:84%
it's a particularly
compelling one.

00:22:04.660 --> 00:22:07.470 line:84%
You know, we didn't
have a healthy attitude

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:09.120 line:84%
about women who
were experiencing

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:10.930 line:90%
violence in the home.

00:22:10.930 --> 00:22:13.000 line:84%
Throughout most of
the '60s and '70s,

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:15.240 line:84%
we were indifferent
to that victimization.

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:18.690 line:84%
We blamed people for
being in relationships

00:22:18.690 --> 00:22:20.680 line:90%
where they were being abused.

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:22.300 line:84%
We joked about
domestic violence.

00:22:22.300 --> 00:22:24.810 line:84%
There used to be a show -- you
remember The Jackie Gleason

00:22:24.810 --> 00:22:28.890 line:84%
Show, at the end of that show,
he'd say, "to the moon, Alice."

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:30.060 line:90%
And everybody would laugh.

00:22:30.060 --> 00:22:31.950 line:84%
And that threat of
violence wasn't something

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:34.110 line:90%
that we saw problematic.

00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:34.710 line:90%
Right.

00:22:34.710 --> 00:22:37.140 line:84%
And then we started telling
stories that actually took

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:39.780 line:84%
the perspective of abused
women, and Farrah Fawcett

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:41.460 line:84%
made that movie,
The Burning Bed,

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:43.020 line:90%
she played the role of that.

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:45.270 line:84%
And then all of a sudden,
people who were being abused

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:47.310 line:84%
started lifting their
voices, and the narrative

00:22:47.310 --> 00:22:48.240 line:90%
began to shift.

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:50.790 line:84%
And you could no longer
ignore the pain and anguish

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:53.910 line:84%
and suffering connected
to that kind of violence

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:55.530 line:90%
and victimization.

00:22:55.530 --> 00:22:58.440 line:84%
Mothers Against Drunk
Driving organized in the '80s

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:01.320 line:84%
to create a new relationship
to driving while drunk.

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:04.977 line:84%
We didn't punish people who
were intoxicated while driving.

00:23:04.977 --> 00:23:06.810 line:84%
Depending on their
status, we might not even

00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:08.610 line:84%
arrest them or
give them a ticket.

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:10.590 line:90%
We didn't deal with it.

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:12.270 line:84%
But when Mothers
Against Drunk Driving

00:23:12.270 --> 00:23:14.910 line:84%
started using narrative
to tell painful stories

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:18.690 line:84%
about the children they lost
because of our inability

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:22.350 line:84%
to confront that problem,
the narrative shifted.

00:23:22.350 --> 00:23:25.830 line:84%
And today, we're seeing
the consequences of that.

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:28.380 line:84%
You can be a celebrity
or athlete --

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:30.820 line:84%
if there's a credible
allegation of domestic violence,

00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:33.555 line:84%
you are going to be
held accountable in ways

00:23:33.555 --> 00:23:35.430 line:84%
that you would not have
been held accountable

00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:36.610 line:90%
even 10 years ago.

00:23:36.610 --> 00:23:38.730 line:90%
And that's a narrative shift.

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:41.310 line:84%
We wouldn't have marriage
equality in this country

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:42.960 line:84%
if we hadn't started
telling stories

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:46.020 line:84%
that got people who didn't
want to hear about same sex

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:49.230 line:84%
relationships to now be in
stories where that became

00:23:49.230 --> 00:23:50.430 line:90%
a part of it.

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:53.670 line:10%
And that's why I think that
Hollywood has a critical role

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:57.630 line:10%
to play in addressing this
long history of silence

00:23:57.630 --> 00:24:00.990 line:10%
about racial inequality,
this long history of silence

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:04.260 line:10%
about racism and bigotry
and racial hierarchy.

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:07.110 line:84%
I look at the catalog
of films and stories

00:24:07.110 --> 00:24:09.810 line:84%
that we have about the
Holocaust, which are so vital.

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:12.180 line:84%
We need more of them, because
clearly, anti-Semitism

00:24:12.180 --> 00:24:13.750 line:90%
is still a problem.

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:15.940 line:84%
We have a whole
catalog of stories.

00:24:15.940 --> 00:24:18.300 line:84%
And I've seen a hundred
films about that experience

00:24:18.300 --> 00:24:21.450 line:84%
and I want to see more,
but we have so few

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:24.210 line:84%
in the catalog of
films and storytelling

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:27.750 line:84%
about the brutality of
slavery, about the era

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:31.650 line:84%
of lynching, about the pain
and suffering of segregation.

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:34.530 line:84%
And I think until we
fix that, until we

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:37.290 line:84%
correct that, these are
American stories that have never

00:24:37.290 --> 00:24:38.970 line:90%
been told.

00:24:38.970 --> 00:24:42.060 line:84%
We just put out a report about
Reconstruction in America --

00:24:42.060 --> 00:24:43.130 line:90%
horrific time.

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:47.070 line:84%
Between 1865 and 1876, 2,000
Black people are lynched,

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:48.690 line:90%
lawlessness reigns.

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:51.090 line:84%
The federal government
retreats from helping

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:52.500 line:90%
formerly enslaved people.

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:55.080 line:84%
It's probably the most
critical 12 year period

00:24:55.080 --> 00:24:58.590 line:84%
in American history and
you can't count on one hand

00:24:58.590 --> 00:25:03.510 line:84%
the number of committed
narratives, films, stories,

00:25:03.510 --> 00:25:06.450 line:84%
programs that have dealt
honestly with that era.

00:25:06.450 --> 00:25:08.873 line:90%
And until we fix that --

00:25:08.873 --> 00:25:10.290 line:84%
and I think that's
what we can do.

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:14.490 line:84%
I think we can invite reluctant
cautious viewers and people who

00:25:14.490 --> 00:25:17.085 line:84%
don't want to, to
step into a space,

00:25:17.085 --> 00:25:18.960 line:84%
and then hear some things
and see some things

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:20.850 line:84%
and live through the
characters in ways that

00:25:20.850 --> 00:25:23.730 line:84%
get them to come out
on the other side

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:25.140 line:90%
now in a different place.

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.150 line:84%
I've seen it happen
with Just Mercy.

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:28.780 line:84%
I saw it happen with
The Color Purple.

00:25:28.780 --> 00:25:32.280 line:84%
I remember seeing The
Color Purple in the South,

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:34.590 line:84%
and there were people all
around me, white viewers,

00:25:34.590 --> 00:25:36.000 line:90%
who were very hesitant.

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:37.590 line:84%
They were told it
was a great movie,

00:25:37.590 --> 00:25:39.990 line:84%
but they weren't so
sure if they wanted.

00:25:39.990 --> 00:25:42.555 line:84%
And, you know, there
were those scenes,

00:25:42.555 --> 00:25:44.430 line:84%
and they were crying
just like everybody else

00:25:44.430 --> 00:25:45.430 line:90%
at the end of that film.

00:25:45.430 --> 00:25:47.940 line:84%
And it changed their
perspective on what

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:51.210 line:84%
it meant to live in that
region with this population

00:25:51.210 --> 00:25:53.370 line:84%
of Black people who were
marginalized and excluded

00:25:53.370 --> 00:25:54.570 line:90%
and oppressed.

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:57.250 line:84%
And that's the power of
effective storytelling.

00:25:57.250 --> 00:25:59.760 line:84%
And that's why I do think
Hollywood has a critical role

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:03.817 line:84%
to play in addressing that very
problem that you articulate,

00:26:03.817 --> 00:26:06.150 line:84%
which is how do we get the
people who are very hesitant,

00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:07.290 line:90%
who are reluctant.

00:26:07.290 --> 00:26:09.720 line:84%
We get to them by going to
their spaces, their living

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:12.270 line:84%
rooms, their movie
theaters, the places where

00:26:12.270 --> 00:26:13.890 line:90%
they consume entertainment.

00:26:13.890 --> 00:26:17.070 line:84%
And getting them exposed
to these realities.

00:26:17.070 --> 00:26:20.780 line:84%
And is it also making
people aware of --

00:26:20.780 --> 00:26:23.860 line:84%
we have a lot of movies
about American history.

00:26:23.860 --> 00:26:26.670 line:84%
We have a ton of movies
about American history.

00:26:26.670 --> 00:26:29.820 line:90%
But it's only half of the story.

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:32.290 line:84%
It's only a quarter
of the story.

00:26:32.290 --> 00:26:36.120 line:84%
All of these amazing films
that people have made from time

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:40.710 line:84%
immemorial, from the very
first Lincoln in Illinois

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:43.680 line:84%
to the newest Lincoln
that was made.

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:47.380 line:84%
This is a big portion
of American history.

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:51.000 line:84%
But there are other portions of
American history that you also

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:58.020 line:84%
need to know in order not to
recreate them in real life.

00:26:58.020 --> 00:27:01.650 line:84%
Is that a way to help
people understand

00:27:01.650 --> 00:27:05.110 line:90%
that these stories are ours?

00:27:05.110 --> 00:27:09.220 line:84%
They're not Black stories
-- they're American stories.

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:13.170 line:84%
It's like making 50
films about World War II

00:27:13.170 --> 00:27:14.770 line:84%
and never addressing
the Holocaust.

00:27:14.770 --> 00:27:15.790 line:90%
It's incomplete.

00:27:15.790 --> 00:27:19.300 line:84%
It almost becomes
dishonest to make films

00:27:19.300 --> 00:27:22.150 line:84%
about the battles and the
soldiers fighting on the field

00:27:22.150 --> 00:27:25.900 line:84%
and never acknowledge or
mention the Holocaust.

00:27:25.900 --> 00:27:27.280 line:90%
It's just dishonest.

00:27:27.280 --> 00:27:32.080 line:84%
And we've done that in America
with a lot of our storytelling.

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:34.570 line:10%
We'll tell stories about
mid-19th century history

00:27:34.570 --> 00:27:36.605 line:10%
and never talk about slavery.

00:27:36.605 --> 00:27:38.230 line:84%
Or do it in such a
marginal way that it

00:27:38.230 --> 00:27:39.610 line:90%
seems as some peripheral issue.

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:43.150 line:84%
And that's what you're seeing
in the stories you hear,

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:44.320 line:90%
that's what you think.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:45.850 line:84%
We tell stories
about World War I,

00:27:45.850 --> 00:27:48.670 line:84%
we tell stories about the
first half of the 20th century

00:27:48.670 --> 00:27:51.820 line:84%
as if we're living
in this place where

00:27:51.820 --> 00:27:55.000 line:84%
there isn't all of this
terror and violence going on.

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:58.150 line:84%
And so yes, I do
think it becomes

00:27:58.150 --> 00:28:01.750 line:84%
dishonest to tell these
stories without being

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:05.080 line:84%
open to and committed
to acknowledging

00:28:05.080 --> 00:28:06.190 line:90%
all the elements of it.

00:28:06.190 --> 00:28:11.080 line:84%
And I just think again,
a lot of storytelling

00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:12.610 line:90%
is what do people want to hear.

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:14.110 line:84%
We're only going
to tell the stories

00:28:14.110 --> 00:28:17.140 line:84%
that people want to hear and
that's how we make money.

00:28:17.140 --> 00:28:19.330 line:84%
If we just give
people what they want.

00:28:19.330 --> 00:28:21.440 line:84%
But when you look at
the film industry,

00:28:21.440 --> 00:28:25.720 line:84%
we have actually
rejected that many times.

00:28:25.720 --> 00:28:27.160 line:84%
People started
making movies that

00:28:27.160 --> 00:28:31.980 line:84%
pushed back against the
bigotry toward LGBTQ people.

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:34.960 line:84%
We started making movies that
pushed back against this idea

00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:38.610 line:84%
that women were just
ornaments in the household.

00:28:38.610 --> 00:28:41.290 line:84%
And it was courageous,
but it was necessary,

00:28:41.290 --> 00:28:44.953 line:84%
and it ultimately was very
effective, and very successful.

00:28:44.953 --> 00:28:46.870 line:84%
And now I actually think
there's a real hunger

00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:49.430 line:84%
to understand the
world in which we live.

00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:52.210 line:84%
We have to match that appetite
with the kind of storytelling

00:28:52.210 --> 00:28:52.780 line:90%
--

00:28:52.780 --> 00:28:54.850 line:84%
I do think as an advocate,
I know I can't go

00:28:54.850 --> 00:28:57.422 line:84%
into a courtroom, and just
demand things, and say,

00:28:57.422 --> 00:28:58.630 line:90%
I want this, and I believe --

00:28:58.630 --> 00:29:00.220 line:90%
I've got to go where people are.

00:29:00.220 --> 00:29:00.720 line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:02.720 line:90%
But I go there with a mission.

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:05.590 line:84%
Of leading them to some
place I think we all

00:29:05.590 --> 00:29:07.480 line:90%
need to be that's healthier.

00:29:07.480 --> 00:29:10.600 line:84%
And that's why we're doing a
lot of the work that we do.

00:29:10.600 --> 00:29:12.880 line:84%
We have a project
where we're actually --

00:29:12.880 --> 00:29:15.880 line:84%
we have memos that talk
about white Southerners that

00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:18.520 line:84%
were abolitionists that
tried to end slavery.

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:20.770 line:84%
We have a memo that talks
about white Southerners that

00:29:20.770 --> 00:29:22.450 line:84%
were opposed to
lynching and tried

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:24.680 line:84%
to prevent these instances
of racial terror.

00:29:24.680 --> 00:29:26.680 line:84%
There are white people
who were actually working

00:29:26.680 --> 00:29:29.050 line:90%
to end racial segregation.

00:29:29.050 --> 00:29:31.810 line:84%
And if I took that memo and
read out some of those names,

00:29:31.810 --> 00:29:34.500 line:84%
mostly nobody in the country
would know any of those names.

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:35.110 line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:35.110 --> 00:29:38.590 line:84%
And I'm saying to folks we don't
have to make false monuments

00:29:38.590 --> 00:29:41.140 line:84%
to people who experimented
on Black women mercilessly

00:29:41.140 --> 00:29:43.840 line:84%
or people who perpetrated and
defended slavery or people who

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:47.320 line:84%
were violent misogynists -- we
don't have to have monuments

00:29:47.320 --> 00:29:49.920 line:84%
to them to have a history
that we can be proud of.

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:52.420 line:84%
There are these other people
who did these honorable things,

00:29:52.420 --> 00:29:56.440 line:84%
and that's the kind of thing
that storytelling can do.

00:29:56.440 --> 00:30:01.690 line:84%
The film about the Black women
who participated in the NASA

00:30:01.690 --> 00:30:02.920 line:90%
launch with John Glenn --

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:07.783 line:84%
that story was made
and new characters

00:30:07.783 --> 00:30:09.700 line:84%
were introduced to the
American consciousness.

00:30:09.700 --> 00:30:11.710 line:84%
It has a profound
impact on how we

00:30:11.710 --> 00:30:15.100 line:84%
think about the lives of
Black women and the value

00:30:15.100 --> 00:30:17.290 line:84%
that Black women can
bring to everything.

00:30:17.290 --> 00:30:19.750 line:84%
But without those stories,
that doesn't happen.

00:30:19.750 --> 00:30:23.930 line:84%
And also, we're talking about
World War I and World War II,

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:29.530 line:84%
and when you think about how
many folks of color fought

00:30:29.530 --> 00:30:33.700 line:84%
in both of those wars, came
home and could not walk

00:30:33.700 --> 00:30:35.740 line:90%
on the sidewalk --

00:30:35.740 --> 00:30:39.373 line:84%
for me, if you're going to tell
the history of World War II,

00:30:39.373 --> 00:30:41.540 line:84%
there were a lot of people
fighting in World War II.

00:30:41.540 --> 00:30:43.870 line:84%
There were a lot of
people who discovered what

00:30:43.870 --> 00:30:46.030 line:90%
was going on in Nazi Germany.

00:30:46.030 --> 00:30:51.280 line:84%
And several of the
Black details were

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:55.940 line:84%
the guys who first walked
into places like Auschwitz.

00:30:55.940 --> 00:30:58.030 line:84%
This is a story that's
never been told.

00:30:58.030 --> 00:31:02.980 line:90%
We are in a way, held apart.

00:31:02.980 --> 00:31:06.400 line:84%
Like we weren't there,
we weren't fighting.

00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:10.660 line:84%
And I feel like a lot
of what is happening

00:31:10.660 --> 00:31:13.300 line:84%
is young folks don't really
know the history because they

00:31:13.300 --> 00:31:17.150 line:84%
think of it as being
something so long ago.

00:31:17.150 --> 00:31:22.390 line:84%
They're not aware of our
part in this country.

00:31:22.390 --> 00:31:26.110 line:84%
Or the Native Americans'
part in this country, what

00:31:26.110 --> 00:31:31.510 line:84%
these folks did, what we did to
fight for a nation that did not

00:31:31.510 --> 00:31:36.100 line:84%
have enough time to show
us a modicum of respect.

00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:41.020 line:84%
And in fact, hung us because
they were mad that we're

00:31:41.020 --> 00:31:42.380 line:90%
wearing medals and stuff.

00:31:42.380 --> 00:31:45.570 line:84%
So those conversations,
those films.

00:31:45.570 --> 00:31:49.020 line:84%
And those are important
and need to be done.

00:31:49.020 --> 00:31:49.910 line:90%
They need to be done.

00:31:49.910 --> 00:31:53.290 line:84%
There's a great book called
Destined to Witness--

00:31:53.290 --> 00:31:55.870 line:84%
Growing up Black
in Nazi Germany.

00:31:55.870 --> 00:31:59.290 line:90%
Extraordinary book.

00:31:59.290 --> 00:32:02.350 line:84%
And we had it for a while, and
when we'd bring it to people,

00:32:02.350 --> 00:32:04.910 line:84%
people would say,
well, what's the hook?

00:32:04.910 --> 00:32:09.220 line:90%
It's like, OK, OK.

00:32:09.220 --> 00:32:14.750 line:84%
Because it's like if you
can't figure out the hook,

00:32:14.750 --> 00:32:16.510 line:84%
I don't know how to
explain it to you.

00:32:16.510 --> 00:32:21.250 line:84%
Or you want to say,
hey, there's a lot

00:32:21.250 --> 00:32:25.330 line:84%
to be learned from Black
performers in the earlier

00:32:25.330 --> 00:32:26.690 line:90%
part of the century.

00:32:26.690 --> 00:32:31.570 line:84%
There's a lot to be learned
from Native American comics.

00:32:31.570 --> 00:32:35.410 line:84%
I mean, there's just Asian
comics and Latin comics,

00:32:35.410 --> 00:32:40.390 line:84%
and we are all here trying
to tell these stories.

00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:47.420 line:84%
And how do we get these
conversations going and get

00:32:47.420 --> 00:32:52.010 line:84%
past the verbiage
that makes it sound --

00:32:52.010 --> 00:32:55.842 line:84%
it sounds good, but you know
that nothing's going to happen.

00:32:55.842 --> 00:32:56.550 line:90%
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:32:59.542 --> 00:33:01.750 line:84%
I think it's really in the
richness of the narrative.

00:33:01.750 --> 00:33:05.750 line:84%
And you just characterized
it so wonderfully.

00:33:05.750 --> 00:33:08.480 line:84%
All of these stories, you
have hundreds of thousands

00:33:08.480 --> 00:33:11.660 line:84%
of Black people who go
fight for an America

00:33:11.660 --> 00:33:14.060 line:84%
that won't let them
vote, that won't

00:33:14.060 --> 00:33:16.220 line:84%
let them go through
the front door, that

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:19.350 line:84%
won't respect their basic
dignity, that won't pay them

00:33:19.350 --> 00:33:19.850 line:90%
fairly.

00:33:19.850 --> 00:33:22.040 line:84%
But they put on a
uniform to go fight

00:33:22.040 --> 00:33:24.560 line:84%
for that nation with
the hope that if they

00:33:24.560 --> 00:33:27.680 line:84%
show courage and bravery,
they'll be accepted.

00:33:27.680 --> 00:33:30.110 line:84%
And then they come
back and they get

00:33:30.110 --> 00:33:32.270 line:84%
to the train
station in some town

00:33:32.270 --> 00:33:36.050 line:84%
where white people will
say, "take that uniform off,

00:33:36.050 --> 00:33:37.550 line:90%
because you don't deserve it."

00:33:37.550 --> 00:33:40.160 line:84%
And they're humiliated
by these mobs.

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:43.520 line:84%
And we tell the stories of all
of these Black veterans that

00:33:43.520 --> 00:33:46.070 line:84%
are lynched and beaten
and abused because they

00:33:46.070 --> 00:33:48.080 line:90%
keep their uniform on.

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:51.800 line:84%
And there's so much important
drama and important truth

00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:55.580 line:84%
telling in a story like that
that we haven't explored,

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:58.310 line:90%
we haven't talked about.

00:33:58.310 --> 00:34:02.150 line:84%
You talk about the Native
contributions, the Code

00:34:02.150 --> 00:34:05.480 line:84%
Breakers and all of that,
and we'll hint at it,

00:34:05.480 --> 00:34:08.949 line:84%
but we haven't actually dealt
honestly with that legacy,

00:34:08.949 --> 00:34:09.449 line:90%
as well.

00:34:09.449 --> 00:34:14.179 line:84%
And I think part of it
is truth can be powerful.

00:34:14.179 --> 00:34:16.550 line:84%
Overcoming obstacles can
be powerful, no matter what

00:34:16.550 --> 00:34:18.590 line:90%
your background is.

00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:21.770 line:84%
A story about perseverance,
a story about people

00:34:21.770 --> 00:34:24.380 line:84%
who have courage, who stand
up when everybody says

00:34:24.380 --> 00:34:27.260 line:84%
sit down, who speak when
everybody says be quiet--

00:34:27.260 --> 00:34:29.674 line:84%
a story like that
can inspire everybody

00:34:29.674 --> 00:34:33.110 line:84%
and not just people who
are part of that community.

00:34:33.110 --> 00:34:37.190 line:84%
And we haven't actually
tapped in to the power

00:34:37.190 --> 00:34:40.880 line:84%
of storytelling, the power of
inspirational storytelling.

00:34:40.880 --> 00:34:43.880 line:84%
The power of these
incredible stories

00:34:43.880 --> 00:34:46.752 line:84%
by denying or not allowing
ourselves to tell stories

00:34:46.752 --> 00:34:48.335 line:84%
about this community
or that community

00:34:48.335 --> 00:34:50.659 line:84%
or these kinds of
difficult topics.

00:34:50.659 --> 00:34:53.690 line:84%
And I just think that's got
to be part of the equation.

00:34:53.690 --> 00:34:55.820 line:84%
We've got to have the
courage of our convictions.

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:59.870 line:84%
But because Hollywood
and story filmmakers

00:34:59.870 --> 00:35:02.690 line:84%
are responsible for a
lot of the false ideas,

00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:05.300 line:84%
a lot of the bigotry, a lot
of that racial hierarchy,

00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:08.150 line:84%
I think we have an
obligation to do it.

00:35:08.150 --> 00:35:10.760 line:84%
And to be courageous
and to push it.

00:35:10.760 --> 00:35:14.420 line:84%
And I just think that's got
to be part of the mix, too.

00:35:14.420 --> 00:35:15.890 line:90%
I came up -- again --

00:35:15.890 --> 00:35:18.450 line:84%
my faith tradition is
to whom much is given,

00:35:18.450 --> 00:35:20.270 line:90%
much is required.

00:35:20.270 --> 00:35:25.280 line:84%
Hollywood, this industry,
has as a central platform

00:35:25.280 --> 00:35:27.200 line:90%
across the globe.

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:30.260 line:84%
We all love movies, we all love
stories, we all love engaging.

00:35:30.260 --> 00:35:33.500 line:84%
And that's an opportunity
to do some things.

00:35:33.500 --> 00:35:36.740 line:84%
And I just think with
that opportunity,

00:35:36.740 --> 00:35:39.410 line:84%
with all of those
resources and spaces,

00:35:39.410 --> 00:35:42.140 line:84%
we have an obligation to
kind of push some things.

00:35:42.140 --> 00:35:45.498 line:84%
And I don't think we should
think about it as corruptive

00:35:45.498 --> 00:35:46.415 line:90%
or anything like that.

00:35:46.415 --> 00:35:47.810 line:90%
I just think it's truth telling.

00:35:47.810 --> 00:35:50.300 line:84%
I'm seeing these
new sets of stories

00:35:50.300 --> 00:35:54.320 line:84%
about challenging the
way people in Hollywood

00:35:54.320 --> 00:35:56.470 line:84%
were targeted during
the McCarthy era.

00:35:56.470 --> 00:35:59.420 line:84%
And there is a narrative now
about the victimization that

00:35:59.420 --> 00:36:03.990 line:84%
took place by the excess
of government oversight

00:36:03.990 --> 00:36:04.785 line:90%
and bigotry.

00:36:04.785 --> 00:36:06.750 line:84%
And when you begin
to understand it,

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:08.570 line:84%
it positions you
differently when

00:36:08.570 --> 00:36:11.300 line:84%
people start making unfair
judgments about people

00:36:11.300 --> 00:36:13.110 line:90%
in this industry today.

00:36:13.110 --> 00:36:14.600 line:84%
And building that
consciousness is

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:18.450 line:84%
how we protect ourselves from
repeating the sins of the past.

00:36:18.450 --> 00:36:20.540 line:84%
And I just think we haven't
done that enough when

00:36:20.540 --> 00:36:22.610 line:84%
it comes to racism,
when it comes to bias.

00:36:22.610 --> 00:36:26.772 line:84%
The beautiful thing about all
that wonderful storytelling

00:36:26.772 --> 00:36:27.730 line:90%
around the Holocaust --

00:36:27.730 --> 00:36:32.030 line:84%
I go to the Holocaust
museum, it's so powerful.

00:36:32.030 --> 00:36:35.780 line:10%
At the end of it, I'm
motivated to say, never again.

00:36:35.780 --> 00:36:37.875 line:84%
I look for cultural
spaces that talk honestly

00:36:37.875 --> 00:36:40.250 line:84%
about the history of slavery
and lynching and segregation

00:36:40.250 --> 00:36:41.698 line:90%
and I can't find them.

00:36:41.698 --> 00:36:44.240 line:84%
Until we create those spaces
and we create those stories that

00:36:44.240 --> 00:36:47.300 line:84%
motivate us at the end to say,
never again can we tolerate

00:36:47.300 --> 00:36:49.020 line:84%
this kind of
bigotry, we're going

00:36:49.020 --> 00:36:53.360 line:84%
to be vulnerable to the problems
that we're seeing today.

00:36:53.360 --> 00:36:58.250 line:84%
I look around and I think,
"where are the LGBTQ stories?"

00:36:58.250 --> 00:37:02.990 line:84%
Where are those stories,
which we need to know,

00:37:02.990 --> 00:37:10.350 line:84%
because people know who they
are when they're little.

00:37:10.350 --> 00:37:15.750 line:84%
So why aren't we supporting
that and allowing films

00:37:15.750 --> 00:37:19.950 line:84%
to be made that will help
young people through?

00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:25.950 line:84%
That will give us some other
visual of the LGBTQ community

00:37:25.950 --> 00:37:28.790 line:90%
other than as the comic relief?

00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:30.373 line:90%
Yeah.

00:37:30.373 --> 00:37:32.040 line:84%
These are really
important, and I always

00:37:32.040 --> 00:37:33.790 line:84%
get in trouble, because
people always say,

00:37:33.790 --> 00:37:35.940 line:90%
well, you never include women.

00:37:35.940 --> 00:37:41.310 line:84%
I do include women, but I
don't include all women.

00:37:41.310 --> 00:37:45.190 line:84%
Because for me, there
was always someone who

00:37:45.190 --> 00:37:47.580 line:90%
was going to win Best Actress.

00:37:47.580 --> 00:37:50.190 line:84%
Always, she was
always going to win.

00:37:50.190 --> 00:37:55.610 line:84%
But Hattie McDaniel was the
first one of color to win,

00:37:55.610 --> 00:37:57.940 line:90%
and she won Best Supporting.

00:37:57.940 --> 00:38:02.880 line:84%
But women excelled -- white
women seem to have had an upper

00:38:02.880 --> 00:38:05.370 line:90%
hand for the longest time.

00:38:05.370 --> 00:38:10.020 line:84%
But even as we look at that,
as we see what we've had to pay

00:38:10.020 --> 00:38:15.360 line:84%
for, because there are not a
whole bunch of female directors

00:38:15.360 --> 00:38:16.627 line:90%
or producers --

00:38:16.627 --> 00:38:17.460 line:90%
and there should be.

00:38:17.460 --> 00:38:22.560 line:84%
There should be, given how
many women we're working

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:24.750 line:90%
in the '20s, '30s, '40s, '50s--

00:38:24.750 --> 00:38:26.850 line:84%
there should be a
plethora and there's not.

00:38:26.850 --> 00:38:29.530 line:90%
So clearly, there is a problem.

00:38:29.530 --> 00:38:32.790 line:84%
And this is one of the
things I've been pushing --

00:38:32.790 --> 00:38:35.670 line:84%
I think institutions and
industries like the film

00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:41.010 line:84%
industry could gain something
making a formal commitment

00:38:41.010 --> 00:38:43.080 line:84%
to truth-telling So
we're going to actually

00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:44.820 line:84%
have a period of
time where we're

00:38:44.820 --> 00:38:46.987 line:84%
going to create a process,
where we're going to just

00:38:46.987 --> 00:38:52.240 line:84%
be honest about the ways that
we failed to confront racism.

00:38:52.240 --> 00:38:56.150 line:84%
We failed to confront
truth telling

00:38:56.150 --> 00:38:58.950 line:90%
from the experience of LGBTQ.

00:38:58.950 --> 00:39:00.660 line:84%
And you can point
to a whole catalog

00:39:00.660 --> 00:39:04.140 line:84%
of films that really
perpetrated a kind of violence

00:39:04.140 --> 00:39:07.320 line:84%
against these communities by
the way they told stories.

00:39:07.320 --> 00:39:09.760 line:84%
And I think it's worth
talking about that.

00:39:09.760 --> 00:39:12.240 line:84%
I actually don't think
it's a waste of time

00:39:12.240 --> 00:39:14.610 line:84%
to engage in that
kind of reflection

00:39:14.610 --> 00:39:16.500 line:84%
and the barriers
that were created,

00:39:16.500 --> 00:39:19.020 line:84%
that barred opportunities
for women directors and women

00:39:19.020 --> 00:39:21.720 line:84%
producers and producers
and directors of color.

00:39:21.720 --> 00:39:23.880 line:84%
You had to be so exceptional
and so extraordinary

00:39:23.880 --> 00:39:26.550 line:84%
to have any chance at
all to direct a film

00:39:26.550 --> 00:39:28.950 line:90%
if you're a Black person.

00:39:28.950 --> 00:39:31.920 line:84%
All of those things are things
I think we can talk about

00:39:31.920 --> 00:39:33.450 line:84%
and should talk
about, because once

00:39:33.450 --> 00:39:35.700 line:84%
we put it all out there--
the truth and reconciliation

00:39:35.700 --> 00:39:39.420 line:84%
process in South Africa was
critical to that nation's

00:39:39.420 --> 00:39:42.270 line:90%
recovery from apartheid.

00:39:42.270 --> 00:39:45.583 line:84%
And victims of apartheid
could come in and speak

00:39:45.583 --> 00:39:46.500 line:90%
what happened to them.

00:39:46.500 --> 00:39:48.120 line:84%
It would be
emotional, but it made

00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:50.190 line:84%
them feel differently
about the future

00:39:50.190 --> 00:39:51.950 line:90%
that they had that opportunity.

00:39:51.950 --> 00:39:54.960 line:84%
And the perpetrators had
to speak to that, as well.

00:39:54.960 --> 00:39:57.000 line:84%
And Rwanda, after
the genocide, they

00:39:57.000 --> 00:39:58.820 line:84%
recognized that
they couldn't heal

00:39:58.820 --> 00:40:00.570 line:84%
if they didn't create
a process for people

00:40:00.570 --> 00:40:02.862 line:84%
to talk honestly about the
pain and suffering of seeing

00:40:02.862 --> 00:40:05.750 line:84%
your whole family or your
whole village slaughtered.

00:40:05.750 --> 00:40:07.470 line:84%
And then for perpetrators
to give voice

00:40:07.470 --> 00:40:09.670 line:84%
to what was going
through their head,

00:40:09.670 --> 00:40:11.770 line:84%
it allowed people
to feel differently.

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:15.960 line:84%
I look at Germany today,
and I wouldn't go to Germany

00:40:15.960 --> 00:40:18.597 line:84%
if they didn't
acknowledge the Holocaust.

00:40:18.597 --> 00:40:19.680 line:90%
If they weren't committed.

00:40:19.680 --> 00:40:21.540 line:84%
And you can't go
200 meters in Berlin

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:23.123 line:84%
without seeing markers
and stones that

00:40:23.123 --> 00:40:25.290 line:84%
had been placed next to the
homes of Jewish families

00:40:25.290 --> 00:40:27.030 line:84%
that were abducted
during the Holocaust.

00:40:27.030 --> 00:40:28.738 line:84%
There's that beautiful
Holocaust memorial

00:40:28.738 --> 00:40:32.310 line:84%
in the center of Berlin, and
cab drivers and hotel operators

00:40:32.310 --> 00:40:33.450 line:90%
encourage you to go there.

00:40:33.450 --> 00:40:35.310 line:84%
Every police officer
in Germany has

00:40:35.310 --> 00:40:37.470 line:90%
to go to the Holocaust sites.

00:40:37.470 --> 00:40:39.720 line:90%
Every student has to go.

00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:42.062 line:84%
There are no Adolf Hitler
statues in Germany.

00:40:42.062 --> 00:40:44.020 line:84%
It would be unconscionable
for someone to say--

00:40:44.020 --> 00:40:45.350 line:90%
That is not there.

00:40:45.350 --> 00:40:46.050 line:90%
Exactly.

00:40:46.050 --> 00:40:49.200 line:84%
It's not waving and people
aren't flying over there.

00:40:49.200 --> 00:40:51.280 line:90%
In fact, the swastika is banned.

00:40:51.280 --> 00:40:51.780 line:90%
Yes.

00:40:51.780 --> 00:40:54.120 line:84%
The Nationalist movement that
is trying to emerge there,

00:40:54.120 --> 00:40:55.800 line:84%
they're using the
Confederate flag

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:59.480 line:90%
from America as their temple.

00:40:59.480 --> 00:41:02.148 line:84%
But the idea that they would
have a monument to an architect

00:41:02.148 --> 00:41:02.940 line:90%
of the Third Reich.

00:41:02.940 --> 00:41:04.020 line:90%
It's so unconscionable.

00:41:04.020 --> 00:41:06.630 line:84%
But you come to this
country and our landscape

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:09.270 line:84%
is littered with the
iconography of the defenders

00:41:09.270 --> 00:41:11.040 line:90%
and perpetrators of slavery.

00:41:11.040 --> 00:41:14.730 line:84%
That's a function of the truth
telling we have failed to do.

00:41:14.730 --> 00:41:16.890 line:84%
And that's, to me,
the beauty of this --

00:41:16.890 --> 00:41:19.020 line:84%
is that on the
other side of this,

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:21.690 line:84%
something really
extraordinary can happen.

00:41:21.690 --> 00:41:23.730 line:84%
So they are telling
me that we're

00:41:23.730 --> 00:41:29.020 line:84%
moments away from
questions, which

00:41:29.020 --> 00:41:34.200 line:90%
I'm excited to see and hear.

00:41:34.200 --> 00:41:37.830 line:84%
I'm not sure how
that's going to work.

00:41:37.830 --> 00:41:41.820 line:84%
I think Shawn is going
to put questions up

00:41:41.820 --> 00:41:42.975 line:90%
that people may have.

00:41:45.810 --> 00:41:49.290 line:84%
So while I'm waiting
for that, I will ask,

00:41:49.290 --> 00:41:52.470 line:84%
are you optimistic
about the future, truly?

00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:53.920 line:90%
I am, I am.

00:41:53.920 --> 00:41:59.430 line:84%
And for me, being hopeful is a
necessary element of this work.

00:41:59.430 --> 00:42:02.790 line:84%
I think hopelessness is
the enemy of justice,

00:42:02.790 --> 00:42:04.810 line:84%
because when you become
hopeless, you accept it.

00:42:04.810 --> 00:42:06.930 line:84%
You don't fight, you
don't tolerate things.

00:42:06.930 --> 00:42:10.170 line:84%
I think injustice prevails
where hopelessness persists.

00:42:10.170 --> 00:42:11.970 line:84%
So for me, it's an
orientation, it's

00:42:11.970 --> 00:42:13.470 line:90%
an obligation to stay hopeful.

00:42:13.470 --> 00:42:17.520 line:84%
But I'm genuinely hopeful,
because I'm seeing some things

00:42:17.520 --> 00:42:18.720 line:90%
that I haven't seen before.

00:42:18.720 --> 00:42:20.700 line:84%
I'm seeing this broad
coalition of people

00:42:20.700 --> 00:42:21.990 line:90%
speaking about these issues.

00:42:21.990 --> 00:42:24.690 line:84%
I'm seeing companies
responding to this.

00:42:24.690 --> 00:42:26.040 line:90%
Even from five years ago.

00:42:26.040 --> 00:42:30.090 line:84%
I'm seeing the things that I
didn't see in 2015 and 2014,

00:42:30.090 --> 00:42:32.200 line:90%
and that encourages me.

00:42:32.200 --> 00:42:33.810 line:90%
But yeah, I am hopeful.

00:42:33.810 --> 00:42:35.770 line:84%
And I live in
Montgomery, Alabama

00:42:35.770 --> 00:42:38.880 line:84%
and it's hard to
not be hopeful when

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:41.707 line:84%
you think about the
generation that came before,

00:42:41.707 --> 00:42:44.040 line:84%
and you've been here, and
you've made that amazing movie

00:42:44.040 --> 00:42:45.540 line:90%
about the boycott.

00:42:45.540 --> 00:42:50.670 line:84%
But when I think about the Black
folks in this community who

00:42:50.670 --> 00:42:55.290 line:84%
did so much more with so much
less, a community of people who

00:42:55.290 --> 00:42:57.660 line:84%
didn't have legal
institutions to protect them,

00:42:57.660 --> 00:42:59.328 line:84%
who didn't have
money to get bail,

00:42:59.328 --> 00:43:00.870 line:84%
that didn't have
all of these things.

00:43:00.870 --> 00:43:03.770 line:84%
And yet they would put
on their Sunday best,

00:43:03.770 --> 00:43:06.840 line:84%
and they would go places and
demand the right to vote,

00:43:06.840 --> 00:43:09.270 line:84%
and fight for the right
to be treated fairly.

00:43:09.270 --> 00:43:11.730 line:84%
And they knew they'd get
bloodied and battered

00:43:11.730 --> 00:43:13.810 line:84%
and beaten, but
they went anyway.

00:43:13.810 --> 00:43:16.560 line:84%
When I think about the
kind of courage that took,

00:43:16.560 --> 00:43:19.080 line:84%
I realize that I'm standing
on their shoulders.

00:43:19.080 --> 00:43:19.800 line:90%
Absolutely.

00:43:19.800 --> 00:43:21.090 line:90%
Standing on their shoulders.

00:43:21.090 --> 00:43:23.132 line:10%
I don't have the choice
to say, this is too hard,

00:43:23.132 --> 00:43:24.590 line:10%
this is too much,
I got to give up.

00:43:24.590 --> 00:43:28.160 line:84%
I just feel like it is an
obligation to keep fighting.

00:43:28.160 --> 00:43:31.340 line:84%
And so yes, I am hopeful
about what we can do,

00:43:31.340 --> 00:43:33.710 line:84%
and about what we can
achieve in this moment.

00:43:33.710 --> 00:43:34.890 line:90%
I just want to keep pushing.

00:43:34.890 --> 00:43:37.520 line:84%
I want to make sure it's not
a moment, but a movement.

00:43:37.520 --> 00:43:41.533 line:84%
That we actually begin
a process where 12 years

00:43:41.533 --> 00:43:42.950 line:84%
from now, we can
say, look at what

00:43:42.950 --> 00:43:44.420 line:84%
we've done over
the last 12 years

00:43:44.420 --> 00:43:45.587 line:90%
to get to a different place.

00:43:45.587 --> 00:43:46.753 line:90%
That's what I'd love to see.

00:43:46.753 --> 00:43:49.610 line:84%
I'd love to see a whole catalog
of films and stories and movies

00:43:49.610 --> 00:43:52.700 line:84%
12 years from now that
you and I could point to,

00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:56.030 line:84%
this new body of work that
has helped us get people

00:43:56.030 --> 00:43:59.870 line:84%
to understand the
challenges that we face.

00:43:59.870 --> 00:44:03.080 line:84%
One of the great things about
all of this as people are --

00:44:03.080 --> 00:44:05.480 line:84%
and we would talk
about being woke --

00:44:05.480 --> 00:44:08.540 line:84%
I say you may be woke now
-- that's because we let you

00:44:08.540 --> 00:44:09.360 line:90%
sleep.

00:44:09.360 --> 00:44:10.850 line:90%
Yeah, that's right.

00:44:10.850 --> 00:44:15.440 line:84%
So now everybody's up, and
the key is we have to stay up.

00:44:15.440 --> 00:44:16.940 line:90%
We have to stay on top of this.

00:44:16.940 --> 00:44:18.620 line:84%
And this is going
to be slow going.

00:44:18.620 --> 00:44:21.140 line:90%
It's not an easy thing to do.

00:44:21.140 --> 00:44:26.540 line:84%
But I feel like people are
open to the conversation.

00:44:26.540 --> 00:44:30.830 line:84%
They're open to hearing
how can I make it better.

00:44:30.830 --> 00:44:32.840 line:84%
"What, I need to change
the name of the team?"

00:44:32.840 --> 00:44:34.290 line:90%
Yes, you do.

00:44:34.290 --> 00:44:35.030 line:90%
Yes.

00:44:35.030 --> 00:44:36.520 line:90%
Yes, you do.

00:44:36.520 --> 00:44:42.140 line:84%
And seeing that was, like, OK,
something clearly is happening.

00:44:42.140 --> 00:44:43.548 line:90%
That's right.

00:44:43.548 --> 00:44:45.590 line:84%
Because it's always been
clear to some of us that

00:44:45.590 --> 00:44:48.380 line:84%
that's an offensive name,
but to... recognize it,

00:44:48.380 --> 00:44:48.980 line:90%
that's good.

00:44:48.980 --> 00:44:50.220 line:90%
But it is encouraging.

00:44:50.220 --> 00:44:50.780 line:90%
Yeah.

00:44:50.780 --> 00:44:53.030 line:84%
But it's nice to hear that
they say they want to stop.

00:44:53.030 --> 00:44:57.200 line:84%
So Shawn, I see that you
are about to jump on this.

00:44:57.200 --> 00:44:58.860 line:90%
I am here, Whoopi.

00:44:58.860 --> 00:45:01.100 line:84%
Thank you, Whoopi
and Bryan, both.

00:45:01.100 --> 00:45:03.710 line:84%
This is such an
amazing conversation.

00:45:03.710 --> 00:45:05.330 line:84%
Literally could
listen for hours.

00:45:05.330 --> 00:45:08.130 line:84%
I want to get to some
of our member questions.

00:45:08.130 --> 00:45:09.200 line:90%
OK.

00:45:09.200 --> 00:45:11.540 line:84%
The first one is
for the both of you.

00:45:11.540 --> 00:45:15.330 line:84%
How can we at the Academy
collect and present evidence,

00:45:15.330 --> 00:45:17.660 line:84%
not just words,
but actual numbers,

00:45:17.660 --> 00:45:20.120 line:84%
to the media
financiers that films

00:45:20.120 --> 00:45:24.740 line:84%
with Black, brown,
LGBTQ casts and stories

00:45:24.740 --> 00:45:28.950 line:90%
do not inhibit good box office?

00:45:28.950 --> 00:45:33.660 line:84%
Well, it seems to me
you have to show people.

00:45:33.660 --> 00:45:36.390 line:84%
You have enough
evidence that people

00:45:36.390 --> 00:45:38.340 line:84%
are interested in these
stories, because they

00:45:38.340 --> 00:45:40.980 line:90%
are the universal stories.

00:45:40.980 --> 00:45:43.710 line:84%
These are not
specific to America.

00:45:43.710 --> 00:45:45.450 line:84%
These are not just
American stories.

00:45:45.450 --> 00:45:49.750 line:84%
These are stories that exist
everywhere in the world.

00:45:49.750 --> 00:45:51.630 line:84%
And you put
everything else out, I

00:45:51.630 --> 00:45:54.250 line:84%
don't know why you
wouldn't take a shot

00:45:54.250 --> 00:45:56.070 line:90%
on some of these stories.

00:45:56.070 --> 00:45:57.210 line:90%
I'm sorry, Bryan.

00:45:57.210 --> 00:45:58.590 line:90%
No, no, no --

00:45:58.590 --> 00:46:01.100 line:84%
as something of a bit
of an outsider on this,

00:46:01.100 --> 00:46:06.180 line:84%
I am struck by why there has to
be this higher burden of proof

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:11.040 line:84%
when it comes to the value
of these other stories.

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:16.560 line:84%
Black Panther made more money
than any Marvel film before it.

00:46:16.560 --> 00:46:20.910 line:84%
It smashed records, it
was an international hit.

00:46:20.910 --> 00:46:22.620 line:84%
And yet there's
still the skepticism,

00:46:22.620 --> 00:46:26.100 line:84%
because people just have a hard
time reconciling themselves

00:46:26.100 --> 00:46:27.980 line:90%
with this reality.

00:46:27.980 --> 00:46:30.780 line:84%
That the films that have
had critical success,

00:46:30.780 --> 00:46:35.100 line:84%
films like Moonlight, films like
12 Years a Slave are stories

00:46:35.100 --> 00:46:38.400 line:84%
that ordinarily, you'd see a lot
of people trying to replicate

00:46:38.400 --> 00:46:41.400 line:84%
that, because they want that
same critical success --

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:43.980 line:84%
or that same kind of
financial success.

00:46:43.980 --> 00:46:46.578 line:84%
But it's almost as if the
industry sometimes says,

00:46:46.578 --> 00:46:48.120 line:84%
oh, well, we did
Black Panther, so we

00:46:48.120 --> 00:46:50.662 line:84%
don't have to do anything again
for another five or 10 years.

00:46:50.662 --> 00:46:51.610 line:90%
Or we did that film.

00:46:51.610 --> 00:46:53.710 line:84%
And I think that's
really the problem.

00:46:53.710 --> 00:46:56.820 line:84%
I don't think it's a finance
proof number as much as it's

00:46:56.820 --> 00:46:58.560 line:90%
an orientation number.

00:46:58.560 --> 00:47:00.190 line:90%
These are American stories.

00:47:00.190 --> 00:47:02.100 line:84%
If we want to tell
the story of America,

00:47:02.100 --> 00:47:04.230 line:84%
we have to tell all
of these stories.

00:47:04.230 --> 00:47:06.510 line:84%
And there's never been a
shortage or an unwillingness

00:47:06.510 --> 00:47:08.820 line:84%
to tell an American
story, but we just

00:47:08.820 --> 00:47:12.250 line:90%
have to now open that lens.

00:47:12.250 --> 00:47:14.400 line:84%
I mean, I think
the data is there.

00:47:14.400 --> 00:47:16.680 line:84%
But more than the
data, I just think

00:47:16.680 --> 00:47:20.340 line:84%
there has to be an orientation
that these stories have value.

00:47:20.340 --> 00:47:22.240 line:84%
Yes, they have value
at the box office,

00:47:22.240 --> 00:47:25.080 line:84%
but they have even
broader value beyond that.

00:47:25.080 --> 00:47:26.650 line:90%
And can I add something to that?

00:47:26.650 --> 00:47:29.520 line:84%
Because it would
also be helpful if

00:47:29.520 --> 00:47:34.650 line:84%
people recognized who
is inhabiting the world.

00:47:34.650 --> 00:47:39.240 line:84%
It's not like we're
not everywhere.

00:47:39.240 --> 00:47:41.700 line:90%
We are all over the world.

00:47:41.700 --> 00:47:47.190 line:84%
You'd be surprised to find out
how many different ethnicities

00:47:47.190 --> 00:47:50.790 line:84%
that look like all the
ethnicities you see here

00:47:50.790 --> 00:47:52.410 line:90%
are living all over the world.

00:47:52.410 --> 00:47:54.810 line:84%
So these stories,
they are connected.

00:47:54.810 --> 00:47:57.930 line:84%
And if we make them,
we'll know if they work

00:47:57.930 --> 00:47:59.570 line:90%
and how well they work.

00:47:59.570 --> 00:48:03.660 line:84%
It's somebody has
got to make the step.

00:48:03.660 --> 00:48:04.920 line:90%
Thank you both.

00:48:04.920 --> 00:48:08.220 line:84%
The next question is
for the both of you --

00:48:08.220 --> 00:48:12.270 line:84%
do you believe that it is more
or less difficult for Black

00:48:12.270 --> 00:48:15.210 line:84%
or people of color,
filmmakers, to break into genre

00:48:15.210 --> 00:48:19.320 line:84%
filmmakers-- example, rom-coms
or sci-fi, fantasy, action,

00:48:19.320 --> 00:48:19.910 line:90%
et cetera?

00:48:26.310 --> 00:48:30.120 line:90%
I don't know, because --

00:48:30.120 --> 00:48:31.640 line:90%
ask Jordan Peele.

00:48:34.170 --> 00:48:38.550 line:84%
He seems to make the
movies he wants to make.

00:48:38.550 --> 00:48:41.640 line:90%
The question is, is there money?

00:48:41.640 --> 00:48:45.870 line:84%
Are there giant bags
of money someplace

00:48:45.870 --> 00:48:49.120 line:84%
for filmmakers who
want to do that?

00:48:49.120 --> 00:48:51.960 line:84%
I feel that's been a lot
of filmmakers' problems,

00:48:51.960 --> 00:48:55.530 line:84%
because if you go to some of
the bigger places, they say,

00:48:55.530 --> 00:48:58.890 line:90%
well, we have to own everything.

00:48:58.890 --> 00:49:04.107 line:84%
You are already in deep
doo before you even start,

00:49:04.107 --> 00:49:05.940 line:84%
because you're just
trying to get some money

00:49:05.940 --> 00:49:07.350 line:90%
to make your movie.

00:49:07.350 --> 00:49:11.100 line:84%
If it was as
prevalent as it seems

00:49:11.100 --> 00:49:13.170 line:84%
to be for some
many other folks, I

00:49:13.170 --> 00:49:17.040 line:84%
think that would answer that
question better than I could.

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:21.480 line:84%
Money is what is at the root
of getting stuff made, I feel.

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:22.950 line:90%
And can I just add --

00:49:22.950 --> 00:49:25.890 line:84%
I actually think when you look
at the history of film making

00:49:25.890 --> 00:49:29.580 line:84%
and storytelling in America
throughout the 20th century,

00:49:29.580 --> 00:49:32.820 line:84%
the industry failed to
actually invite voices in

00:49:32.820 --> 00:49:35.430 line:84%
to tell complete
stories, to tell

00:49:35.430 --> 00:49:39.460 line:84%
truthful stories about our
experience in this country.

00:49:39.460 --> 00:49:42.835 line:84%
And when you have
failed, and you

00:49:42.835 --> 00:49:44.460 line:84%
didn't do what you
were supposed to do,

00:49:44.460 --> 00:49:45.870 line:90%
you should want to repair that.

00:49:45.870 --> 00:49:47.910 line:90%
You should want to remedy that.

00:49:47.910 --> 00:49:50.640 line:84%
And you should actually
make it a commitment

00:49:50.640 --> 00:49:56.580 line:84%
to invest in corrections to
that, and let that lead you.

00:49:56.580 --> 00:49:59.850 line:84%
I just don't think that we
should assume that the playing

00:49:59.850 --> 00:50:02.670 line:84%
field is level
now and we're just

00:50:02.670 --> 00:50:05.290 line:84%
going to see used the same
tactics and strategy that we

00:50:05.290 --> 00:50:06.540 line:90%
used before.

00:50:06.540 --> 00:50:08.280 line:90%
In the 1960s --

00:50:08.280 --> 00:50:09.780 line:84%
what we've done in
this country when

00:50:09.780 --> 00:50:11.280 line:84%
it comes to justice
and rights, it's

00:50:11.280 --> 00:50:14.100 line:84%
like it's different than what
we do in other areas of the law.

00:50:14.100 --> 00:50:16.680 line:84%
In law school, I
had to study damages

00:50:16.680 --> 00:50:19.560 line:84%
for tort violations and
contract violations, corporate.

00:50:19.560 --> 00:50:22.410 line:84%
There's trouble damages
and punitive damages.

00:50:22.410 --> 00:50:24.300 line:84%
We have a very
elaborate construct

00:50:24.300 --> 00:50:26.608 line:84%
of what people are
obligated to pay when they

00:50:26.608 --> 00:50:27.900 line:90%
have violated someone's rights.

00:50:27.900 --> 00:50:30.540 line:84%
But in the human rights, in
the civil rights context,

00:50:30.540 --> 00:50:32.310 line:84%
it's almost as if we
say all we want to do

00:50:32.310 --> 00:50:34.235 line:84%
is to stop violating
their rights

00:50:34.235 --> 00:50:35.860 line:84%
and you don't have
to do anything else.

00:50:35.860 --> 00:50:37.950 line:84%
And I think that's left
us vulnerable to failing

00:50:37.950 --> 00:50:42.062 line:84%
to appreciate our obligations
to do more to repair

00:50:42.062 --> 00:50:43.020 line:90%
the damage that we did.

00:50:43.020 --> 00:50:46.250 line:84%
I think in 1965, after
these states denied

00:50:46.250 --> 00:50:48.762 line:84%
Black people the right
to vote for 100 years,

00:50:48.762 --> 00:50:50.220 line:84%
states like Alabama
and Mississippi

00:50:50.220 --> 00:50:52.740 line:84%
should have just been told,
"you can't do that anymore."

00:50:52.740 --> 00:50:53.820 line:84%
I actually think
that Black people

00:50:53.820 --> 00:50:55.528 line:84%
should have been
automatically registered

00:50:55.528 --> 00:50:56.730 line:90%
to vote in those states.

00:50:56.730 --> 00:50:58.438 line:84%
I don't think that
would have been wrong.

00:50:58.438 --> 00:51:01.448 line:84%
It would have been repair
for the damage that was done.

00:51:01.448 --> 00:51:03.240 line:84%
I don't think it would've
been wrong to say

00:51:03.240 --> 00:51:04.620 line:84%
to the University of
Georgia and Mississippi,

00:51:04.620 --> 00:51:06.540 line:84%
you've got to let Black
kids who are qualified

00:51:06.540 --> 00:51:09.510 line:84%
to attend these universities,
attend for free,

00:51:09.510 --> 00:51:12.150 line:84%
because you have to do something
to repair the damage that you

00:51:12.150 --> 00:51:14.980 line:84%
did by excluding Black
people for so long.

00:51:14.980 --> 00:51:17.905 line:90%
And so this industry, I think --

00:51:17.905 --> 00:51:19.530 line:84%
it would not be wrong
for this industry

00:51:19.530 --> 00:51:23.280 line:84%
to say, "we're going to commit
a percentage of our resources

00:51:23.280 --> 00:51:27.240 line:84%
to create platforms
for Black storytellers,

00:51:27.240 --> 00:51:29.695 line:84%
and producers, and directors
and, people of color,

00:51:29.695 --> 00:51:32.070 line:84%
and people whose voices have
been historically excluded."

00:51:32.070 --> 00:51:34.220 line:84%
Because we now want
to say to the world

00:51:34.220 --> 00:51:36.900 line:84%
that we want to fix the
problem that we helped

00:51:36.900 --> 00:51:39.060 line:90%
sustain by our silence.

00:51:39.060 --> 00:51:41.260 line:84%
And with that kind
of orientation,

00:51:41.260 --> 00:51:44.430 line:84%
we can make it easier for
directors and producers

00:51:44.430 --> 00:51:46.483 line:84%
and storytellers of color
to have an opportunity

00:51:46.483 --> 00:51:47.400 line:90%
to tell their stories.

00:51:47.400 --> 00:51:51.150 line:84%
And what we'll get from that are
some amazingly talented people

00:51:51.150 --> 00:51:54.180 line:84%
becoming some of our leaders
and some of the legends

00:51:54.180 --> 00:51:56.145 line:84%
that we all want to
be in these spaces.

00:51:56.145 --> 00:51:58.020 line:84%
I don't think that's
the wrong orientation --

00:51:58.020 --> 00:52:00.103 line:84%
I think it could actually
be a healthy orientation

00:52:00.103 --> 00:52:00.850 line:90%
for the industry.

00:52:00.850 --> 00:52:03.800 line:90%
That's a great idea, too.

00:52:03.800 --> 00:52:04.950 line:90%
Thank you.

00:52:04.950 --> 00:52:06.920 line:84%
Next question --
we have two more --

00:52:06.920 --> 00:52:09.380 line:84%
Just Mercy -- this
is for you, Bryan.

00:52:09.380 --> 00:52:12.290 line:84%
Just Mercy resonates with
our experiences with access

00:52:12.290 --> 00:52:15.380 line:84%
to justice in my
homeland, Kenya.

00:52:15.380 --> 00:52:17.510 line:84%
How do we get the
critics on board

00:52:17.510 --> 00:52:21.440 line:84%
so that they highlight
diverse stories?

00:52:21.440 --> 00:52:24.620 line:90%
It's interesting --

00:52:24.620 --> 00:52:26.912 line:90%
I don't know how to do --

00:52:26.912 --> 00:52:29.120 line:84%
I think one of the things
that was fascinating to me,

00:52:29.120 --> 00:52:31.162 line:84%
and I didn't understand
this, and Whoopi probably

00:52:31.162 --> 00:52:33.050 line:84%
had this experience
much more than I have,

00:52:33.050 --> 00:52:37.460 line:84%
is that the people who were
judging the film initially

00:52:37.460 --> 00:52:40.940 line:84%
were people who knew very little
about the lived experience.

00:52:40.940 --> 00:52:44.072 line:84%
It's like we played the
film and Black audiences

00:52:44.072 --> 00:52:45.530 line:84%
and white audiences
were responding

00:52:45.530 --> 00:52:47.437 line:90%
with such enthusiasm.

00:52:47.437 --> 00:52:49.520 line:84%
You look at the cinema
score for a film like that,

00:52:49.520 --> 00:52:51.540 line:90%
it's through the roof.

00:52:51.540 --> 00:52:53.490 line:84%
But critics all went,
we've seen this before.

00:52:53.490 --> 00:52:55.490 line:84%
And I was, like, well,
where have you seen this?

00:52:55.490 --> 00:52:58.100 line:84%
I don't recall any films
that actually talk --

00:52:58.100 --> 00:52:59.360 line:90%
I didn't see it before.

00:52:59.360 --> 00:53:02.450 line:84%
I never met a lawyer until
I got to Harvard Law School,

00:53:02.450 --> 00:53:05.120 line:84%
so a story of Black people
just surrounded by lawyers,

00:53:05.120 --> 00:53:07.190 line:90%
that wasn't familiar to me.

00:53:07.190 --> 00:53:09.980 line:84%
And I think there is a
sort of cynicism about it.

00:53:09.980 --> 00:53:11.830 line:84%
I remember when The
Color Purple came out,

00:53:11.830 --> 00:53:15.470 line:84%
I remember reading a review
in the Atlanta Constitution

00:53:15.470 --> 00:53:18.300 line:84%
by a well-known reviewer
and what they said,

00:53:18.300 --> 00:53:20.420 line:84%
well, they didn't
like parts of film.

00:53:20.420 --> 00:53:22.910 line:84%
They said it was unfair
to the white people.

00:53:22.910 --> 00:53:24.800 line:84%
There were no white
heroes in this.

00:53:24.800 --> 00:53:26.800 line:84%
They didn't present
white people.

00:53:26.800 --> 00:53:28.940 line:84%
And it's that kind
of orientation

00:53:28.940 --> 00:53:31.280 line:84%
that I think we
have to confront.

00:53:31.280 --> 00:53:34.880 line:84%
There's a critical perspective
in this country that

00:53:34.880 --> 00:53:37.010 line:84%
is shaped by these
same narratives

00:53:37.010 --> 00:53:39.950 line:84%
that we've been talking about
during this conversation.

00:53:39.950 --> 00:53:41.130 line:90%
We have to understand that.

00:53:41.130 --> 00:53:43.005 line:84%
And I think people
need to be called out.

00:53:43.005 --> 00:53:45.380 line:84%
I remember writing-- that was
the first time I ever wrote

00:53:45.380 --> 00:53:47.760 line:90%
a letter to a film critic --

00:53:47.760 --> 00:53:51.173 line:84%
is when I read that review
about The Color Purple --

00:53:51.173 --> 00:53:52.340 line:90%
it wasn't fair to the white.

00:53:52.340 --> 00:53:54.340 line:10%
As if somehow white people
were doing everything

00:53:54.340 --> 00:53:57.170 line:10%
they possibly could in the
first half of the 20th century

00:53:57.170 --> 00:54:00.440 line:10%
to help Black people escape
the ravages of sharecropping

00:54:00.440 --> 00:54:01.700 line:10%
and tenant farming?

00:54:01.700 --> 00:54:05.240 line:84%
If anything, it
understated multiple ways

00:54:05.240 --> 00:54:07.760 line:84%
in which a white bias
and Jim Crow laws

00:54:07.760 --> 00:54:10.010 line:84%
could create that
kind of narrative.

00:54:10.010 --> 00:54:13.010 line:84%
And I just think part
of it is shifting

00:54:13.010 --> 00:54:14.930 line:84%
who has that platform,
just like we need

00:54:14.930 --> 00:54:18.080 line:84%
a more diverse
community of people

00:54:18.080 --> 00:54:22.790 line:84%
making films and producing
films and starring in films,

00:54:22.790 --> 00:54:26.090 line:84%
we need a more diverse platform
of people who are evaluating

00:54:26.090 --> 00:54:28.610 line:84%
these films and they
can speak to the value

00:54:28.610 --> 00:54:30.500 line:90%
of the storytelling.

00:54:30.500 --> 00:54:33.120 line:90%
And I think that's part of it.

00:54:33.120 --> 00:54:36.050 line:84%
And I'm glad to hear
that somebody from Kenya

00:54:36.050 --> 00:54:39.710 line:84%
is here, because I do think
these stories are global.

00:54:39.710 --> 00:54:41.930 line:84%
I've been to Africa
and seeing the way

00:54:41.930 --> 00:54:45.840 line:84%
in which access to justice can
be a problem in that society.

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:48.560 line:84%
When you hear a story about how
you can overcome that and maybe

00:54:48.560 --> 00:54:50.953 line:84%
succeed, that's
inspiring for everybody.

00:54:50.953 --> 00:54:52.370 line:84%
I've gotten some
beautiful letters

00:54:52.370 --> 00:54:55.150 line:84%
from people in East
Africa in response

00:54:55.150 --> 00:54:57.590 line:84%
to seeing the film that
I was really moved by.

00:54:57.590 --> 00:55:01.130 line:84%
And that's the power of
effective storytelling.

00:55:01.130 --> 00:55:02.210 line:90%
Thank you so much, Bryan.

00:55:02.210 --> 00:55:07.100 line:84%
And our last question is,
we are poor at teaching

00:55:07.100 --> 00:55:09.290 line:90%
our history of discrimination.

00:55:09.290 --> 00:55:12.440 line:84%
While docs -- documentaries
-- are a good start,

00:55:12.440 --> 00:55:16.370 line:84%
how do we encourage filmmakers
to eschew creating distorted

00:55:16.370 --> 00:55:19.730 line:84%
history or social views in
the name of creative choice?

00:55:25.070 --> 00:55:28.110 line:84%
I'm gonna let you
do this one, Bryan.

00:55:28.110 --> 00:55:29.460 line:90%
You know, it's funny --

00:55:29.460 --> 00:55:34.500 line:84%
I think most
filmmakers don't want

00:55:34.500 --> 00:55:38.730 line:84%
to present a story
that they know is going

00:55:38.730 --> 00:55:40.500 line:90%
to be exposed as false, right?

00:55:40.500 --> 00:55:42.720 line:84%
So I was in a movie
theater one time

00:55:42.720 --> 00:55:48.460 line:84%
and they had a character in
Mobile, Alabama and the actor

00:55:48.460 --> 00:55:50.550 line:84%
said, I'm going to
Mobile, Alabama.

00:55:50.550 --> 00:55:53.770 line:84%
And the whole theater started
laughing and a bunch of people

00:55:53.770 --> 00:55:56.200 line:84%
left because they
couldn't trust a film that

00:55:56.200 --> 00:55:57.480 line:90%
didn't get that right.

00:55:57.480 --> 00:56:00.270 line:84%
And so if we really
want to be honest,

00:56:00.270 --> 00:56:02.820 line:84%
if we don't want to expose
ourselves as just being

00:56:02.820 --> 00:56:03.840 line:90%
completely --

00:56:03.840 --> 00:56:08.040 line:84%
then I think we have to be
willing to tell the full story.

00:56:08.040 --> 00:56:13.950 line:84%
And I often think
that filmmakers have

00:56:13.950 --> 00:56:15.820 line:90%
one kind of viewer in mind.

00:56:15.820 --> 00:56:17.730 line:84%
That's a certain person
of a certain race

00:56:17.730 --> 00:56:19.170 line:84%
of a certain
demographic group as

00:56:19.170 --> 00:56:20.910 line:90%
if America is only that person.

00:56:20.910 --> 00:56:22.860 line:84%
And as Whoopi said,
we're going to soon

00:56:22.860 --> 00:56:24.530 line:90%
be a minority majority country.

00:56:24.530 --> 00:56:26.940 line:84%
There will be more people
of color in this country

00:56:26.940 --> 00:56:29.322 line:84%
than there are people
who identify as white.

00:56:29.322 --> 00:56:30.780 line:84%
And I just think
when you step back

00:56:30.780 --> 00:56:33.563 line:84%
and say, let me think about
how young kids of color

00:56:33.563 --> 00:56:34.980 line:84%
are going to
experience this story

00:56:34.980 --> 00:56:37.300 line:84%
or how women are going
to experience this,

00:56:37.300 --> 00:56:39.750 line:84%
then you begin to understand
that these so-called creative

00:56:39.750 --> 00:56:42.930 line:84%
choices kind of make it easy
and comfortable for people

00:56:42.930 --> 00:56:44.430 line:84%
are things that we
should push back.

00:56:44.430 --> 00:56:46.305 line:84%
The most effective films
that I've ever seen,

00:56:46.305 --> 00:56:48.120 line:84%
the most moving
films are the films

00:56:48.120 --> 00:56:50.520 line:84%
that at times made me
uncomfortable, because that's

00:56:50.520 --> 00:56:51.930 line:90%
what good storytelling does.

00:56:51.930 --> 00:56:53.040 line:90%
It actually pushes you.

00:56:53.040 --> 00:56:54.630 line:84%
It actually exposes
you to a thing.

00:56:54.630 --> 00:56:56.548 line:84%
It's not just
something that you take

00:56:56.548 --> 00:56:57.840 line:90%
to make you feel good at night.

00:56:57.840 --> 00:57:01.380 line:84%
It's sometimes the most powerful
filmmaking can push you.

00:57:01.380 --> 00:57:05.010 line:84%
We cannot create more justice,
we cannot create a healthier

00:57:05.010 --> 00:57:08.880 line:84%
society if we're unwilling to
do things that are uncomfortable

00:57:08.880 --> 00:57:10.200 line:90%
and inconvenient.

00:57:10.200 --> 00:57:11.280 line:90%
And powerful films--

00:57:11.280 --> 00:57:13.030 line:84%
I think about films
like Schindler's List.

00:57:13.030 --> 00:57:15.120 line:84%
At times, it's so
uncomfortable and so painful.

00:57:15.120 --> 00:57:17.670 line:84%
You're worried about
what's going to happen.

00:57:17.670 --> 00:57:18.770 line:90%
You know, great films --

00:57:18.770 --> 00:57:21.000 line:84%
Fruitvale Station, my friend,
Ryan Coogler's film --

00:57:21.000 --> 00:57:22.890 line:90%
difficult, challenging.

00:57:22.890 --> 00:57:24.690 line:10%
But those are the
kinds of stories

00:57:24.690 --> 00:57:26.970 line:10%
that ultimately stay with you.

00:57:26.970 --> 00:57:29.580 line:84%
I can remember those
scenes, even though I

00:57:29.580 --> 00:57:30.990 line:90%
saw the film 20 years ago.

00:57:30.990 --> 00:57:33.210 line:84%
If you want to make
memorable film making that

00:57:33.210 --> 00:57:36.450 line:84%
makes a difference, that lasts,
that endures the test of time,

00:57:36.450 --> 00:57:38.850 line:84%
then we have to be
willing to tell the truth.

00:57:38.850 --> 00:57:40.720 line:84%
We have to be willing
to do the hard things,

00:57:40.720 --> 00:57:43.330 line:84%
even if they're uncomfortable
and inconvenient.

00:57:43.330 --> 00:57:46.780 line:84%
And I think that ought to be the
norm rather than the exception.

00:57:46.780 --> 00:57:48.930 line:84%
And I think Hollywood
and the big studios

00:57:48.930 --> 00:57:50.940 line:84%
have to keep reinforcing
their interest

00:57:50.940 --> 00:57:53.880 line:84%
in that kind of storytelling
by investing in films that

00:57:53.880 --> 00:57:56.940 line:84%
tell those stories, and not
just the simple easy stories

00:57:56.940 --> 00:57:59.610 line:84%
that you can forget about an
hour or two after you leave

00:57:59.610 --> 00:58:00.930 line:90%
the theater.

00:58:00.930 --> 00:58:04.920 line:84%
I also think that it would
behoove folks who wanted

00:58:04.920 --> 00:58:10.230 line:84%
to tell these stories who may
not be Native American or Black

00:58:10.230 --> 00:58:13.320 line:84%
folks -- they may be white
people who are just invested

00:58:13.320 --> 00:58:14.460 line:90%
in the story --

00:58:14.460 --> 00:58:19.200 line:84%
team up with folks so that we
can help you get them right.

00:58:19.200 --> 00:58:22.680 line:84%
We can help you get
to the place where

00:58:22.680 --> 00:58:23.910 line:90%
you want your story to be.

00:58:23.910 --> 00:58:32.250 line:84%
I believe we can do this
as a coalition of artists,

00:58:32.250 --> 00:58:34.920 line:90%
because that's what we are.

00:58:34.920 --> 00:58:38.460 line:84%
We're artists, whether we're
hair artists or light artists,

00:58:38.460 --> 00:58:40.590 line:90%
we are all artists in this.

00:58:40.590 --> 00:58:44.280 line:84%
And together, we can
make huge differences.

00:58:44.280 --> 00:58:47.200 line:84%
But we've got to cop to
the stuff we don't know.

00:58:47.200 --> 00:58:49.740 line:84%
Talk about the stuff
that really pisses us off

00:58:49.740 --> 00:58:51.150 line:90%
that we don't understand.

00:58:51.150 --> 00:58:53.710 line:90%
And listen, I'm not perfect.

00:58:53.710 --> 00:58:57.750 line:84%
I know I have all kinds of
tendencies to snap in my mind

00:58:57.750 --> 00:59:01.950 line:84%
when I see somebody and
they become that so and so.

00:59:01.950 --> 00:59:04.080 line:84%
We all have these
tendencies, and so we

00:59:04.080 --> 00:59:07.050 line:84%
have to fight through them
and talk about the fact

00:59:07.050 --> 00:59:09.570 line:84%
that we have them,
and then move forward.

00:59:09.570 --> 00:59:13.800 line:84%
I think we can only do it
kind of collectively, I think,

00:59:13.800 --> 00:59:15.300 line:90%
really.

00:59:15.300 --> 00:59:16.490 line:90%
Yeah.

00:59:16.490 --> 00:59:21.840 line:84%
Bryan and Whoopi Goldberg, thank
you both so much for joining,

00:59:21.840 --> 00:59:25.110 line:84%
for sharing your truth, your
transparency, your lights

00:59:25.110 --> 00:59:26.350 line:90%
with all of us.

00:59:26.350 --> 00:59:28.320 line:84%
To our members who
have been attending

00:59:28.320 --> 00:59:31.230 line:84%
from around the world, to
the board of governors,

00:59:31.230 --> 00:59:33.910 line:84%
and to the incredible Academy
staff who put this together,

00:59:33.910 --> 00:59:35.340 line:84%
this is just the
beginning of what

00:59:35.340 --> 00:59:38.430 line:84%
we hope to be a long lasting
fruitful conversation

00:59:38.430 --> 00:59:40.110 line:90%
about change.

00:59:40.110 --> 00:59:42.780 line:84%
Thank you for hosting our
first Academy Dialogues.

00:59:42.780 --> 00:59:45.090 line:84%
We are better because
of the both of you.

00:59:45.090 --> 00:59:47.510 line:90%
Thank you so very much.

00:59:47.510 --> 00:59:49.390 line:90%
Thank you.

